[LINK: (annexation of Crimea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annexation_of_Crimea_by_the_Russian_Federation]

[LINK: (Donbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_in_Donbas]

[LINK: (Kerch Strait) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerch_Strait_incident]

[LINK: (2022 invasion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] 
 [LINK: (prelude) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_to_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]

[LINK: (prelude) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_to_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]

On 24 February 2022,  [LINK: (Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia]  invaded  [LINK: (Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine] , starting the largest and deadliest war in Europe since  [LINK: (World War II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II] . It is a major escalation of the  [LINK: (war between the two countries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_War]  that began when Russia attacked Ukraine  [LINK: (in 2014) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war#History] . [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-17]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-18]  [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-19]  The fighting has caused hundreds of thousands of  [LINK: (military casualties) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casualties_of_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War]  and tens of thousands of Ukrainian  [LINK: (civilian casualties) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attacks_on_civilians_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] . As of December 2025, Russian troops  [LINK: (occupy almost 20% of Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian-occupied_territories_of_Ukraine] . [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-20]  From a population of 41 million, about 8 million Ukrainians have been internally displaced [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-21]  and 6–7 million have  [LINK: (fled the country) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukrainian_refugee_crisis] , [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-22]  creating Europe's  [LINK: (largest refugee crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_refugee_crises]  since World War II.

In late 2021, Russia  [LINK: (massed troops near Ukraine's borders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prelude_to_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  and  [LINK: (issued demands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Russian_ultimatum_to_NATO]  to  [LINK: (the West) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_world] , including a ban on Ukraine ever joining  [LINK: (NATO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO] . [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-23]  [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-v440-24]  After repeatedly denying having plans to attack Ukraine, on 24 February 2022, Russian president  [LINK: (Vladimir Putin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Putin]   [LINK: (announced) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putin%27s_invasion_of_Ukraine_speech]  a " [LINK: (special military operation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_military_operation] ", saying that it was to support the Russian-backed breakaway  [LINK: (republics of Donetsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk_People%27s_Republic]  and  [LINK: (Luhansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luhansk_People%27s_Republic] , whose  [LINK: (paramilitary forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_separatist_forces_in_Ukraine]  had been fighting Ukraine in the  [LINK: (Donbas War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_in_Donbas]  since 2014. Putin espoused  [LINK: (irredentist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_irredentism]  and  [LINK: (imperialist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_neo-imperialism]  views  [LINK: (challenging Ukraine's legitimacy as a state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_to_exist] ,  [LINK: (baselessly claimed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disinformation_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine#Russian_themes]  that the Ukrainian government were  [LINK: (neo-Nazis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Far-right_politics_in_Ukraine#Russian_disinformation]  committing  [LINK: (genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegations_of_genocide_in_Donbas]  against the  [LINK: (Russian minority) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russians_in_Ukraine]  in the  [LINK: (Donbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donbas] , and said that Russia's goal was to "demilitarise and denazify" Ukraine. [LINK: ([d]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-30]  Russian air strikes and a ground  [LINK: (invasion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  were launched on a  [LINK: (northern front) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Ukraine_campaign]  from  [LINK: (Belarus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belarus]  towards the capital  [LINK: (Kyiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyiv] , a  [LINK: (southern front) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Ukraine_campaign]  from occupied  [LINK: (Crimea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimea] , and an  [LINK: (eastern front) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Ukraine_campaign]  from the Donbas towards  [LINK: (Kharkiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kharkiv] . Ukraine enacted  [LINK: (martial law) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martial_law_in_Ukraine] , ordered  [LINK: (a general mobilisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobilization_in_Ukraine] , and severed diplomatic  [LINK: (relations with Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia%E2%80%93Ukraine_relations] .

Russian troops retreated from the north and the outskirts of Kyiv by April 2022, after encountering stiff resistance and logistical challenges. The  [LINK: (Bucha massacre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucha_massacre]  was uncovered after their withdrawal. Russia launched an  [LINK: (offensive in the Donbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Donbas_(2022)]  and captured  [LINK: (Mariupol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariupol]  after  [LINK: (a destructive siege) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Mariupol] . Russia continued to bomb military and civilian targets far from the front, and  [LINK: (struck Ukraine's energy grid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_strikes_against_Ukrainian_infrastructure_(2022%E2%80%93present)]  during winter months. In late 2022, Ukraine launched successful counteroffensives in  [LINK: (the south) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Kherson_counteroffensive]  and  [LINK: (east) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Kharkiv_counteroffensive] , liberating most of  [LINK: (Kharkiv Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kharkiv_Oblast] . Soon after, Russia illegally  [LINK: (annexed four partly-occupied provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_annexation_of_Donetsk,_Kherson,_Luhansk_and_Zaporizhzhia_oblasts] . In November, Ukraine  [LINK: (liberated the city of Kherson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_of_Kherson]  and all  [LINK: (land west of the Dnipro river) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_occupation_of_Kherson_Oblast] . After small but steady Russian advances in the east in the first half of 2024, Ukraine launched a cross-border  [LINK: (offensive into Russia's Kursk Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kursk_campaign]  in August, where  [LINK: (North Korean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korea]  soldiers were sent to help Russia. The  [LINK: (United Nations Human Rights Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_the_United_Nations_High_Commissioner_for_Human_Rights]  reports that Russia is committing severe  [LINK: (human rights violations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian-occupied_territories_of_Ukraine#Violations_and_war_crimes]  in occupied Ukraine. Russia's attacks on civilians, as well as the policies it has introduced in occupied territories, have led to  [LINK: (allegations of genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegations_of_genocide_of_Ukrainians_in_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War] . [LINK: ([e]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-35]  The direct cost of the war for Russia has been over US$450 billion. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DU-36]  [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-mt20250114-37]  [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-kpost20250115-38]

The Russian invasion was met with  [LINK: (international condemnation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reactions_to_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] . The  [LINK: (UN General Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UN_General_Assembly]  passed  [LINK: (a resolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_General_Assembly_Resolution_ES-11/1]  condemning the invasion and demanding a full Russian withdrawal. The  [LINK: (International Court of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Court_of_Justice]  ordered Russia to halt military operations, and the  [LINK: (Council of Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_Europe]  expelled Russia. Many countries imposed  [LINK: (sanctions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_during_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  on Russia and its ally Belarus while providing large-scale  [LINK: (humanitarian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_humanitarian_aid_to_Ukraine_during_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War]  and  [LINK: (military aid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_military_aid_to_Ukraine_during_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War]  to Ukraine.  [LINK: (Protests occurred) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  around the world, with anti-war  [LINK: (protesters in Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_anti-war_protests_in_Russia]  being met by mass arrests and greater media  [LINK: (censorship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Censorship_in_Russia] . War-related disruption to Ukrainian agriculture and shipping worsened the  [LINK: (world food crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022%E2%80%932023_global_food_crises] ; war-related environmental damage was described as  [LINK: (ecocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecocide#Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] . The  [LINK: (International Criminal Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court]  (ICC) opened  [LINK: (an investigation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court_investigation_in_Ukraine]  into  [LINK: (crimes against humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity] ,  [LINK: (war crimes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_crimes_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] ,  [LINK: (abduction of Ukrainian children) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Child_abductions_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , and genocide against Ukrainians. The ICC issued  [LINK: (arrest warrants for Putin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court_arrest_warrants_for_Russian_leaders]  and five other Russian officials.  [LINK: (Peace negotiations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace_negotiations_in_the_Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)]  have stalled, and Russia has repeatedly refused calls for a ceasefire.


# BACKGROUND #

After the  [LINK: (dissolution of the Soviet Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dissolution_of_the_Soviet_Union]  in 1991, Russia and Ukraine maintained cordial relations. In return for security guarantees, Ukraine signed the  [LINK: (Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_on_the_Non-Proliferation_of_Nuclear_Weapons]  in 1994 and gave up its  [LINK: (nuclear weapons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction] . [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-39]  Russia, the US, and UK agreed in the  [LINK: (Budapest Memorandum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budapest_Memorandum]  to respect Ukraine's sovereignty and borders. [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-40]  In 1999, Russia signed the  [LINK: (Charter for European Security) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charter_for_European_Security] , affirming that every country had the right "to choose or change its security arrangements" and to join military alliances. [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-41]  In 2005, Putin said that if Ukraine wanted to join NATO, "we will respect their choice". [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-42]

In 2013,  [LINK: (Ukraine's parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verkhovna_Rada]  approved finalising the  [LINK: (European Union–Ukraine Association Agreement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union%E2%80%93Ukraine_Association_Agreement] . [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-43]  Russia put pressure on Ukraine to reject the agreement and  [LINK: (imposed economic sanctions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_embargo_of_Ukrainian_goods]  on the country. [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-44]  Kremlin adviser  [LINK: (Sergei Glazyev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Glazyev]  warned in September 2013 that if Ukraine signed the EU agreement, Russia would no longer acknowledge Ukraine's borders. [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-45]

In November, Ukrainian president  [LINK: (Viktor Yanukovych) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Yanukovych]  suddenly withdrew from signing the agreement, [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-46]  choosing closer ties to Russia instead. This coerced withdrawal sparked massive protests known as  [LINK: (Euromaidan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euromaidan] , culminating in the  [LINK: (Revolution of Dignity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolution_of_Dignity]  in February 2014. Almost  [LINK: (100 protesters were killed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maidan_casualties]  by state forces, most of them shot by police snipers. Despite signing  [LINK: (an agreement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agreement_on_settlement_of_political_crisis_in_Ukraine] , Yanukovych secretly fled. Ukraine's parliament then  [LINK: (voted to remove him) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Yanukovych#Removal_from_presidency]  and hold new elections. [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-b713-47]

On 27 February 2014,  [LINK: (Russian soldiers with no insignia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_green_men_(Russo-Ukrainian_War)]  began to  [LINK: (occupy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_occupation_of_Crimea]  the Ukrainian territory of Crimea, [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-48]  blockading Ukrainian military bases. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-49]  Russia  [LINK: (annexed Crimea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annexation_of_Crimea_by_the_Russian_Federation]  in March, after a  [LINK: (widely-condemned and disputed referendum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_Crimean_status_referendum] . Several scholars have likened the annexation to Nazi Germany's  [LINK: (Anschluss) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anschluss]  of Austria. [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-50]  [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-51]  [LINK: ([47]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-z259-52]

[LINK: (Pro-Russian protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_pro-Russian_unrest_in_Ukraine]  followed in the Ukrainian cities of  [LINK: (Donetsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk]  and  [LINK: (Luhansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luhansk] , covertly funded and organised by Russia. [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-53]  In April 2014, armed  [LINK: (Russian paramilitaries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_separatist_forces_in_Donbas]  seized  [LINK: (Sloviansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sloviansk]  and other settlements, proclaiming the  [LINK: (Donetsk People's Republic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk_People%27s_Republic]  (DPR) and  [LINK: (Luhansk People's Republic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luhansk_People%27s_Republic]  (LPR) as independent. Their commander,  [LINK: (Igor Girkin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Igor_Girkin] , acknowledged that this sparked the  [LINK: (War in Donbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_in_Donbas] , as Ukraine soon launched an operation to retake the territory. [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-54]  [LINK: ([50]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Unnamed_War_Sloviansk-55]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-AJ-56]  Russia covertly supported the separatists with troops, tanks and artillery. [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-57]  The  [LINK: (International Criminal Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court_investigation_in_Ukraine]  judged that the war was a national and international armed conflict involving Russia, [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-58]  and the  [LINK: (European Court of Human Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Court_of_Human_Rights]  judged that Russia controlled the DPR and LPR from 2014 onward. [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-59]  Ukraine's parliament declared the Donbas region to be occupied by Russia. [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-60]

The annexation of Crimea and Donbas war sparked a wave of  [LINK: (Russian nationalism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_nationalism] . Analyst  [LINK: (Vladimir Socor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Socor]  called Putin's  [LINK: (2014 speech) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimean_speech_of_Vladimir_Putin]  following the annexation a "manifesto of  [LINK: (Greater-Russia irredentism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_irredentism] ". [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-61]  Putin began referring to " [LINK: (Novorossiya) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novorossiya] " (New Russia), a former Russian imperial territory that covered much of southern Ukraine. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-62]  Russian-backed forces were influenced by  [LINK: (Russian neo-imperialism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_neo-imperialism]  [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-63]  and sought to create a  [LINK: (new Novorossiya) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novorossiya_(confederation)] . [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-64]

When the conflict began in 2014, Ukraine was officially  [LINK: (neutral) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutral_country]  [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Atlantic_Council:_Ukrainian_neutrality-66]  and said it was not seeking NATO membership. [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-67]  [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-68]  Following Russia's occupation of Crimea and  [LINK: (invasion of the Donbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_in_Donbas#August_2014_invasion_by_Russian_forces] , Ukraine's parliament voted in December 2014 to revoke the country's neutral status and seek  [LINK: (NATO membership) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine%E2%80%93NATO_relations] . [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-69]  [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-70]

The  [LINK: (Minsk agreements) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minsk_agreements]  (September 2014 and February 2015) aimed to resolve the conflict, but ceasefires and further negotiations failed. [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-71]  The  [LINK: (West) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_world] 's weak response to Russian actions led Russia to believe the West would not react strongly to the 2022 invasion. [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-72]  [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-73]  [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-74]  Several political scientists said this encouraged further Russian aggression. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-75]  [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-u485-76]  [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-o290-77]

Economic interests were also a motive for Russia's invasion of Ukraine and  [LINK: (annexation of the southeast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_annexation_of_Donetsk,_Kherson,_Luhansk_and_Zaporizhzhia_oblasts] . [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Kirby-78]  [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Brewster-79]

Ukraine holds Europe's second-largest reserves of natural gas, coal, and titanium, and some of the world's largest reserves of iron ore and uranium. [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Kirby-78]  Ukraine is also thought to hold Europe's largest supply of recoverable  [LINK: (rare-earth minerals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rare-earth_minerals] , and one of Europe's largest reserves of lithium. [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Brewster-79]  Furthermore, Ukraine is one of the world's biggest suppliers of wheat, corn and other grains. [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Kirby-78]  Control of lithium deposits in the Donbas, and Ukraine's grain wealth, would give Russia a "monopoly on the world market". [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DD-80]  In 2022, Russian General Vladimir Ovchinsky confirmed that one of the goals of the invasion was to seize Ukrainian lithium deposits. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DU-36]  [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RA-81]  About 80% of  [LINK: (Ukraine's oil) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oil_in_Ukraine] ,  [LINK: (natural gas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_gas_in_Ukraine]  and  [LINK: (coal fields) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coal_in_Ukraine]  are found in the Donbas-Dnipro region. [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Brewster-79]

The Russian elite, especially Russian generals, had invested assets and property in Ukraine for  [LINK: (money laundering) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Money_laundering]  purposes before the invasion. [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DD-80]


# PRELUDE #

There was a Russian military build-up near Ukraine's borders in March and April 2021, [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-82]  and again in both Russia and Belarus from October 2021 onward. [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-83]  Russia said it was only holding  [LINK: (military exercises) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_exercise] . Members of its government, including Putin, repeatedly denied having plans to invade Ukraine, issuing denials up until the day before the invasion. [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-denials-84]  [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Deny-85]  [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Czech-86]

While Russian troops massed on Ukraine's borders,  [LINK: (Russia's proxy forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_separatist_forces_in_Ukraine]  launched thousands of attacks on Ukrainian troops in the Donbas. [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-87]   [LINK: (Observers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OSCE_Special_Monitoring_Mission_to_Ukraine]  from the  [LINK: (Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organization_for_Security_and_Co-operation_in_Europe]  (OSCE) reported more than 90,000 ceasefire violations throughout 2021, most in Russian-controlled territory. [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-88]

In July 2021, Putin published " [LINK: (On the Historical Unity of Russians and Ukrainians) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_the_Historical_Unity_of_Russians_and_Ukrainians] ", in which he called Ukraine "historically Russian lands" and claimed there is "no historical basis" for the "idea of Ukrainian people as a nation separate from the Russians". [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Duben-89]  [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-90]  Putin was accused of promoting  [LINK: (Russian imperialism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_imperialism] , [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-91]  historical revisionism and disinformation. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-92]  [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-93]

The  [LINK: (December 2021 Russian ultimatum to NATO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/December_2021_Russian_ultimatum_to_NATO] , included demands that NATO end all activity in its Eastern European member states and ban Ukraine or any  [LINK: (former Soviet state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-Soviet_states]  from ever joining the alliance. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-v440-24]  [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Reuters_17_Dec_21-94]  [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-FG-95]  Russia's government said NATO was a threat and warned of a military response if it followed an "aggressive line". [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-96]  A US official said the US was willing to discuss the proposals, but there were some that "the Russians know are unacceptable" and had already been ruled out. [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Reuters_17_Dec_21-94]  NATO secretary general  [LINK: (Jens Stoltenberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jens_Stoltenberg]  replied that "Russia has no veto" on whether Ukraine joins, and "has no right to establish a  [LINK: (sphere of influence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sphere_of_influence]  to try to control their neighbours". [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-97]  NATO underlined that it is a  [LINK: (defensive alliance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collective_security] , and had  [LINK: (co-operated with Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia-NATO_relations]  until the Crimea annexation. [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-NATO_myths-98]

Several Western political analysts suggested that Russia knew its "unrealistic demands" would be rejected, [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Reuters_17_Dec_21-94]  giving it a  [LINK: (pretext) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pretext]  to invade. [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-99]  No countries bordering Russia had joined NATO since 2004. Ukraine had not yet applied, and some NATO states would likely veto its membership. [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-100]  Analysts  [LINK: (Taras Kuzio) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taras_Kuzio]  and  [LINK: (Vladimir Socor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Socor]  agree that "when Russia made its decision to invade Ukraine, that country was more remote than ever not only from NATO membership but from any track that might lead to membership". [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-FG-95]  Political scientists  [LINK: (Michael McFaul) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_McFaul]  and Robert Person said Russia's occupation of Crimea and the Donbas had already blocked Ukraine's NATO membership; they suggested Putin's real aim was to subjugate Ukraine. [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Person-McFaul1-101]

NATO offered to negotiate some of Russia's demands and to improve military  [LINK: (transparency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transparency_(behavior)] , as long as Russia stopped its troop buildup. [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-102]  The alliance rejected Russia's demand to keep Ukraine out of NATO forever, pointing out that Russia had signed agreements affirming the right of Ukraine and other countries to join alliances. [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ElPais-103]  [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DD-80]  The US proposed that itself and Russia sign an agreement not to station missiles or troops in Ukraine. [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ElPais-103]  Putin replied that Russia's demands had been "ignored", and the Russian troop buildup continued. [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-104]

Western leaders vowed heavy sanctions should Putin invade rather than negotiate. [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-105]  French president  [LINK: (Emmanuel Macron) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emmanuel_Macron]  [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-106]  and German chancellor  [LINK: (Olaf Scholz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olaf_Scholz]  met Putin in February 2022 to dissuade him from invading. Putin told Scholz that Ukraine should not be an independent state. [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-107]  Zelenskyy said Putin had broken agreements and could not be trusted to respect Ukrainian neutrality. [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-108]  Ukraine had been a neutral country in 2014 when Russia occupied Crimea and  [LINK: (invaded the Donbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_Russian_invasion_of_the_Donbas] . [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Atlantic_Council:_Ukrainian_neutrality-66]  [LINK: ([104]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Chatham_House:_How_to_end_Russia's_war_on_Ukraine-109]  At the  [LINK: (Munich Security Conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich_Security_Conference] , Zelenskyy called for Western powers to end their " [LINK: (appeasement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appeasement] " of Putin and give a timeframe for when Ukraine could join NATO. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-110]

Shortly before the invasion, Russia's proxy forces stepped up attacks on Ukrainian forces and civilians in the Donbas. [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-BBC_19_Feb_2022-111]  [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Guardian_18_Feb_2022-112]  Separatist leaders warned that Ukraine was about to launch an offensive, but they gave no evidence, and  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  noted it would be "exceedingly risky" for Ukraine to assault the Donbas while Russian troops were massed on its borders. [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Guardian_18_Feb_2022-112]  Ukraine and Western leaders accused Russia of staging  [LINK: (false flag) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_flag]  attacks and trying to provoke retaliation, to give Russia a pretext for invading. [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-BBC_19_Feb_2022-111]  [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Guardian_18_Feb_2022-112]  On 17 February, Russian proxy forces  [LINK: (shelled a kindergarten) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanytsia_Luhanska_kindergarten_bombing]  in Ukrainian-held territory, then blamed it on Ukraine. [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Guardian_18_Feb_2022-112]  Zelenskyy said his military would not respond to the provocations. [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-BBC_19_Feb_2022-111]

The  [LINK: (Royal United Services Institute) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_United_Services_Institute]  reported that Russia's plan involved defeating Ukraine within ten days and capturing or killing its government, followed by  [LINK: ("mopping up" operations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zachistka] ; establishing  [LINK: (filtration camps for Ukrainians) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_filtration_camps_for_Ukrainians] ; setting up occupation regimes; executing people involved in the Revolution of Dignity; and annexation. [LINK: ([108]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RUSI_invasion_plan-113]  [LINK: ([109]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-KyivPost_invasion_plan-114]  [LINK: ([110]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-SkyNews_invasion_plan-115]  The decision to invade was reportedly made by Putin and a small group of  [LINK: (war hawks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_hawk]  or  [LINK: (siloviki) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silovik]  in Putin's inner circle, including national security adviser  [LINK: (Nikolai Patrushev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikolai_Patrushev]  and  [LINK: (defence minister) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister_of_Defence_(Russia)]   [LINK: (Sergei Shoigu) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Shoigu] . [LINK: ([111]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-116]

After the invasion began, Ukrainian and Western analysts assessed that Putin seemed to have believed the Russian military could seize  [LINK: (Kyiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyiv]  within days. This assessment led to the conclusion that "taking Kyiv in three days" had been the original goal of the invasion. [LINK: ([112]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Sheftalovich2-117]  [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-France_24-118]  [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-119]

On 21 February, Putin  [LINK: (announced) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putin%27s_recognition_of_the_Donbas_republics]  that Russia recognised the  [LINK: (Donetsk People's Republic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk_People%27s_Republic]  and  [LINK: (Luhansk People's Republic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luhansk_People%27s_Republic]  as independent states. The following day, Russia announced that it was sending troops into the territories as "peacekeepers", [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-120]  and the  [LINK: (Federation Council of Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federation_Council_(Russia)]  authorised the use of military force abroad. [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-121]

Before 5 a.m. Kyiv time on 24 February, Putin, in  [LINK: (another speech) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putin%27s_invasion_of_Ukraine_speech] , announced a " [LINK: (special military operation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_military_operation] ", which effectively declared war on Ukraine. [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Meduza_Putin_announces_invasion-122]  [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-123]  Putin said the operation was to "protect the people" of the Russian-controlled breakaway republics. He  [LINK: (baselessly claimed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegations_of_genocide_in_Donbas]  that Russians in the Donbas had "been facing humiliation and genocide perpetrated by the Kyiv regime" for 8 years. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-The_Conversation:_Baseless_but_not_unprecedented-26]  Putin said Russia was being threatened: he  [LINK: (baselessly claimed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disinformation_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine#Russian_themes]  that Ukrainian officials  [LINK: (were neo-Nazis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disinformation_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine#Allegations_of_Nazism]  under Western control, that Ukraine was developing nuclear weapons, and that a hostile NATO was building up its forces and infrastructure in Ukraine. [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Putin_declaration-124]  [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-bbc22422-125]  He said Russia sought the "demilitarisation and denazification" of Ukraine, and denied the legitimacy of the Ukrainian state. [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-bbc22422-125]  [LINK: ([121]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Time22222-126]  Putin said he had no plans to occupy Ukraine. [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Putin_declaration-124]  The invasion began within minutes of Putin's speech. [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Meduza_Putin_announces_invasion-122]


# EVENTS #

The invasion began at dawn on 24 February. [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Meduza_Putin_announces_invasion-122]  [LINK: ([122]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-127]  It was the biggest attack on a European country and first full-scale war in Europe since World War II. [LINK: ([123]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-128]  Russia launched a simultaneous ground and air attack. [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CriticalThreats-129]  [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_4MAR-130]  Missiles struck targets throughout Ukraine, [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-131]  and Russian troops invaded from the north, east, and south. [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-132]  Russia did not officially declare war. [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-University_of_Hull_2023_u063-133]  Immediately after the invasion began, Zelenskyy declared  [LINK: (martial law in Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martial_law_in_Ukraine]  in a video speech. [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-134]  The same evening, he ordered a  [LINK: (general mobilisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Ukrainian_mobilization]  of all Ukrainian males aged 18–60, [LINK: ([130]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Interfax_2022-135]  prohibiting them from leaving the country. [LINK: ([131]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-136]

The first stage of the invasion was conducted on four fronts: one towards western  [LINK: (Kyiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyiv]  from Belarus by the Russian  [LINK: (Eastern Military District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Military_District] , one deployed towards eastern Kyiv by the  [LINK: (Central Military District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Military_District]  (northeastern front), comprised the  [LINK: (41st Combined Arms Army) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/41st_Combined_Arms_Army]  and the  [LINK: (2nd Guards Combined Arms Army) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Guards_Combined_Arms_Army] , [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TR-137]  one deployed towards Kharkiv, and a fourth, southern front originating in Crimea and Russia's  [LINK: (Rostov oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rostov_Oblast]  with an eastern axis towards Odesa and a western area of operations toward  [LINK: (Mariupol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariupol] . [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TR-137]  The invasion was unexpectedly met by fierce resistance. [LINK: ([133]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-138]  Russia failed to take Kyiv and was repulsed in the battles of  [LINK: (Irpin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Irpin] ,  [LINK: (Hostomel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hostomel] , and  [LINK: (Bucha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bucha] . The Russians tried to encircle the capital, but defenders under  [LINK: (Oleksandr Syrskyi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oleksandr_Syrskyi]  held their ground, effectively using Western  [LINK: (Javelin anti-tank missiles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FGM-148_Javelin]  and  [LINK: (Stinger anti-aircraft missiles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIM-92_Stinger]  to thin Russian supply lines and stall the offensive. [LINK: ([134]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-139]

By 7 April, Russian troops deployed to the northern front pulled back from Kyiv, to resupply and redeploy to the Donbas to reinforce the renewed invasion of southeastern Ukraine. The northeastern front was similarly withdrawn for redeployment to southeastern Ukraine. [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TR-137]  [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-news.yahoo.com-140]  On 26 April, delegates from the US and 40 allied nations  [LINK: (met at Ramstein Air Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Ramstein_Air_Base_meeting]  in Germany to discuss  [LINK: (a coalition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine_Defense_Contact_Group]  to provide economic support, military supplies and refitting to Ukraine. [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-141]  Following Putin's  [LINK: (Victory Day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victory_Day_(9_May)#In_Russia]  speech on 9 May, US Director of National Intelligence  [LINK: (Avril Haines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avril_Haines]  said no short term resolution should be expected. [LINK: ([137]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-142]

Ukraine's reliance on Western-supplied equipment constrained operational effectiveness, as supplying countries feared Ukraine would use Western-made matériel to strike targets in Russia. [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Sabbagh-143]  Experts disagreed on the future of the conflict; some suggested Ukraine should trade territory for peace, [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-144]  others believed Ukraine could maintain its resistance due to Russian losses. [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-145]

The invasion began on 24 February, launched out of Belarus to target Kyiv, and from the northeast against the city of Kharkiv. The southeastern front was conducted as two separate spearheads, from Crimea and the southeast against Luhansk and Donetsk. [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-146]

Russian troops tried to seize  [LINK: (Kyiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyiv]  quickly with a spearhead on 24 February, from Belarus south along the west bank of the  [LINK: (Dnipro River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnipro_River] , with  [LINK: (Spetsnaz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spetsnaz]  infiltrating into the city supported by airborne operations and a rapid mechanised advance from the north, but failed. [LINK: ([142]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-wapo20220320-147]  [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-148]  The apparent intent was to encircle the city from the west, supported by two separate axes of attack along the east bank of the Dnipro: the western at  [LINK: (Chernihiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernihiv] , and from the east at  [LINK: (Sumy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumy] . [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CriticalThreats-129]  [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_4MAR-130]  Russian forces advanced into  [LINK: (Chernihiv Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernihiv_Oblast]  on 24 February, besieging its administrative capital within  [LINK: (four days of fighting) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Chernihiv] . [LINK: ([144]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-149]  [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-150]  Travelling along highways, Russian forces reached  [LINK: (Brovary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brovary] , an eastern suburb of Kyiv, on 4 March. [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_4MAR-130]  [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CriticalThreats-129]

The US contacted Zelenskyy and offered to help him flee, lest the Russian Army attempt to kidnap or kill him; Zelenskyy responded that "The fight is here; I need ammunition, not a ride." [LINK: ([146]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-151]  By early March, Russian advances along the west side of the Dnipro were limited by Ukrainian defences. [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_4MAR-130]  [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CriticalThreats-129]  As of 5 March,  [LINK: (a Russian convoy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Kyiv_Convoy] , reportedly 64 kilometres (40 mi) long, had made little progress toward Kyiv. [LINK: ([147]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-152]  Advances from Chernihiv largely halted as a  [LINK: (siege began there) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Chernihiv] . Russian forces advanced on Kyiv from the northwest, capturing  [LINK: (Bucha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucha,_Kyiv_Oblast] ,  [LINK: (Hostomel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hostomel]  and  [LINK: (Vorzel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vorzel]  by 5 March. [LINK: ([148]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-153]  [LINK: ([149]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-154]  [LINK: ([150]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-155]  By 11 March, the lengthy convoy had largely dispersed and taken cover. [LINK: ([151]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-156]  On 16 March, Ukrainian forces began a counter-offensive. [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-157]  Unable to achieve a quick victory in Kyiv, Russian forces switched to  [LINK: (indiscriminate bombing) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiscriminate_attack]  and  [LINK: (siege) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege]  warfare. [LINK: ([153]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-158]  [LINK: ([154]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-159]

On 25 March, a Ukrainian counter-offensive retook towns to the east and west of Kyiv. [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-160]  [LINK: ([156]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Guardian220325-161]  Russian troops in the Bucha area retreated north. Ukrainian forces entered the city on 1 April, and recaptured the region around Kyiv, and uncovered evidence of  [LINK: (war crimes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_crimes_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  in  [LINK: (Bucha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucha_massacre] . [LINK: ([157]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-162]  [LINK: ([158]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-163]   [LINK: (The Pentagon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pentagon]  confirmed on 6 April that the Russian army had left  [LINK: (Chernihiv Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernihiv_Oblast] ; [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-164]  local authorities said Russian troops had left Sumy Oblast. [LINK: ([160]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-165]  NATO secretary-general Jens Stoltenberg said the Russian "retraction, resupply, and redeployment" of troops from the Kyiv area should be interpreted as an expansion of Putin's plans for concentrating his forces on eastern Ukraine. [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-news.yahoo.com-140]

On 24 February, Russian attack groups moved northwest from Crimea, capturing bridges over the  [LINK: (Dnieper) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnieper]  [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-166]  and the  [LINK: (North Crimean Canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Crimean_Canal] . [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-167]  On 1 March,  [LINK: (Kherson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kherson]  was the first major city to fall to Russia. [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-168]  Russian troops moved on  [LINK: (Mykolaiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mykolaiv]  and  [LINK: (attacked it) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Mykolaiv] , but were repelled. [LINK: ([164]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-169]  Russian forces approached the  [LINK: (Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaporizhzhia_Nuclear_Power_Plant]  [LINK: ([165]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-170]  and  [LINK: (besieged Enerhodar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Enerhodar] . By 25 February, Russian units had begun advancing on  [LINK: (Mariupol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariupol] . [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-171]  Russian forces captured  [LINK: (Berdiansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berdiansk] . [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CNN-Berdyansk-172]  [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-unianberdyansk-173]  [LINK: ([169]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-174]  On 1 March,  [LINK: (Denis Pushilin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denis_Pushilin] , head of the DPR, announced that it had surrounded  [LINK: (Volnovakha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volnovakha] . [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-175]

By 18 March, Mariupol was encircled and fighting had reached the city centre, hampering efforts to evacuate civilians. [LINK: ([171]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-176]  The Russians demanded surrender, and the Ukrainians refused. [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CBC-2022-03-21-177]  On 27 March, Ukrainian deputy prime minister  [LINK: (Olha Stefanishyna) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olha_Stefanishyna]  said that "(m)ore than 85 percent of the whole town is destroyed." [LINK: ([173]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-178]  Russia refused safe passage into Mariupol to 50 buses sent by the UN to evacuate civilians. [LINK: ([174]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-179]  [LINK: ([175]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-180]

In the east, Russian troops  [LINK: (attempted to capture Kharkiv) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kharkiv_(2022)] , [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-181]  and met strong Ukrainian resistance.  [LINK: (Izium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izium]  was captured by Russian forces on 1 April [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-182]  after a monthlong battle. [LINK: ([178]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-183]  On 25 March, the Russian defence ministry said it would seek to occupy major cities in eastern Ukraine. [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-184]

On 3 April, following the retreat of Russian forces from Kyiv, Russia expanded its attack on southern Ukraine, with bombardment and strikes against Odesa, Mykolaiv, and the Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant. [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-185]  [LINK: ([181]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-186]  By 7 April, the renewed massing of Russian troops and tanks around Izium,  [LINK: (Sloviansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sloviansk] , and  [LINK: (Kramatorsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kramatorsk]  prompted Ukrainian officials to advise remaining residents near the eastern border to evacuate to western Ukraine. [LINK: ([182]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-187]

By 17 April, Russian progress on the southeastern front appeared impeded by Ukrainian forces in the large, heavily fortified  [LINK: (Azovstal Iron and Steel Works) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azovstal_Iron_and_Steel_Works]  and surrounding area in Mariupol. [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RNZM-188]  On 19 April Russia launched an "eastern assault" across a 480-kilometre (300 mi) front extending from Kharkiv to Donetsk and Luhansk, with simultaneous missile attacks again directed at Kyiv and Lviv. [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nytimes.com-189]  An anonymous US Defence official called the Russian offensive "minimal at best". [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-190]

By 30 May, disparities between Russian and Ukrainian artillery were apparent, with Ukrainian artillery vastly outgunned. [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Sabbagh-143]  In response to Biden's indication that enhanced artillery would be provided to Ukraine, Putin said Russia would expand its invasion to include new cities, and ordered a missile strike against Kyiv on 6 June after not directly attacking it for weeks. [LINK: ([186]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-191]  On 10 June, deputy head of the  [LINK: (SBU) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_Service_of_Ukraine]  Vadym Skibitsky stated that during the Sievierodonetsk campaign, the frontlines were where the future of the invasion would be decided: [LINK: ([187]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-iktgvs-192]

This is an artillery war now, and we are losing in terms of artillery. Everything now depends on what [the west] gives us. Ukraine has one artillery piece to 10 to 15 Russian artillery pieces. Our western partners have given us about 10% of what they have.

On 29 June, US intelligence director Avril Haines said US agencies agreed the invasion will continue "for an extended period of time ... In short, the picture remains pretty grim and Russia's attitude toward the West is hardening." [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-193]  On 5 July,  [LINK: (BBC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC]  reported that extensive destruction by the invasion would cause immense financial damage to Ukraine's economy, with Ukrainian prime minister  [LINK: (Denys Shmyhal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denys_Shmyhal]  telling a reconstruction conference that Ukraine needed $750B for a recovery plan and Russian oligarchs should contribute. [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-194]

The chief spokesman for the  [LINK: (Russian Ministry of Defence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Ministry_of_Defence]   [LINK: (Igor Konashenkov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Igor_Konashenkov]  revealed that Russian troops were divided between the Army Groups "Centre" commanded by Colonel General  [LINK: (Aleksander Lapin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleksandr_Lapin_(general)]  and "South" commanded by Army General  [LINK: (Sergey Surovikin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergey_Surovikin] . [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Novaya-195]  On 20 July, Lavrov announced that Russia would respond to the increased military aid being received by Ukraine from abroad by expanding its special military operation to  [LINK: (Zaporizhzhia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaporizhzhia_Oblast]  and  [LINK: (Kherson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kherson_Oblast]  oblasts. [LINK: ([191]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-196]   [LINK: (Russian Ground Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Ground_Forces]  started recruiting volunteer battalions to create a new  [LINK: (3rd Army Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3rd_Army_Corps_(Russia)]  within the Western Military District, with a planned strength of 16,000–60,000 personnel. [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-197]  Its units were deployed to the front around the September  [LINK: (Kharkiv counteroffensive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Kharkiv_counteroffensive] , in time to join the Russian retreat. The 3rd Army "melted away" according to  [LINK: (Forbes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes] , having little impact on the battlefield along with other irregular forces. [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-198]

On 13 April, Russian forces intensified their attack on the  [LINK: (Azovstal Iron and Steel Works) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azovstal_Iron_and_Steel_Works] . [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-199]  Ukrainian prime minister Denys Shmyhal said Ukrainian soldiers had vowed to ignore the ultimatum to surrender and fight to the last. [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RNZM-188]  On 20 April, Putin said the siege could be considered tactically complete, since the 500 Ukrainian troops entrenched in bunkers within the ironworks and estimated 1,000 civilians were sealed off from relief. [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-200]

After meetings with Putin and Zelenskyy, UN secretary-general Guterres on 28 April said he would attempt to organise an evacuation from Azovstal in accordance with assurances from Putin. [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-201]  On 30 April, Russian troops allowed civilians to leave under UN protection. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-202]  By 3 May, after allowing 100 civilians to depart, Russian troops renewed their bombardment. [LINK: ([198]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-203]  On 6 May,  [LINK: (The Daily Telegraph) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Daily_Telegraph]  reported that Russia had used thermobaric bombs against the remaining soldiers. Zelenskyy authorised the commander of the steel factory to surrender as necessary. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-204]  On 7 May, all civilians were evacuated. [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-205]

After the last civilians evacuated, 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers remained barricaded there, 700 were injured. They communicated a plea for a military corridor to evacuate, as they expected execution if they surrendered. [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-206]  Reports of dissent on 8 May indicated that the commander of the  [LINK: (Ukrainian marines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukrainian_Marine_Corps]  had made an unauthorised acquisition of tanks, munitions, and personnel, broke out from the position and fled. The remaining soldiers spoke of a weakened defence as a result, which allowed progress to advancing Russian attacks. [LINK: ([202]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-207]   [LINK: (Ilia Somolienko) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illia_Samoilenko_(officer)] , deputy commander of the remaining Ukrainian troops, said: "We are basically here dead men. Most of us know this and it's why we fight so fearlessly." [LINK: ([203]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-208]

On 16 May, the Ukrainian General staff announced that the Mariupol garrison had "fulfilled its combat mission" and final evacuations had begun. 264 service members were evacuated to Olenivka under Russian control, while 53 who were seriously injured were taken to a hospital in  [LINK: (Novoazovsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novoazovsk] , controlled by Russia. [LINK: ([204]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nytimes16-209]  [LINK: ([205]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-210]  Following the evacuation, Russian and DPR forces fully controlled Mariupol. Russia press secretary Dmitry Peskov said Putin had guaranteed that the fighters who surrendered would be treated "in accordance with international standards" while Zelenskyy said that "the work of bringing the boys home continues..." [LINK: ([206]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-reutersmay17-211]

A Russian missile attack on  [LINK: (Kramatorsk railway station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kramatorsk_railway_station]  on 8 April killed at least 61 [LINK: ([207]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-z362-212]  and injuring 87 to 300. [LINK: ([208]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-213]  On 11 April, Zelenskyy said Ukraine expected a new Russian offensive in the east. [LINK: ([209]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-214]  American officials said Russia had withdrawn or been repulsed elsewhere, and was preparing a retraction, resupply, and redeployment to the southeastern Ukraine front. [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-215]  [LINK: ([211]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-216]  Military satellites photographed Russian convoys of infantry and mechanised units deploying south from Kharkiv to Izium on 11 April, apparently part of the planned Russian redeployment. [LINK: ([212]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-217]

On 18 April, with Mariupol overtaken by Russian forces, the Ukrainian government announced that the second phase of the reinforced invasion of the Donetsk, Luhansk and Kharkiv oblasts had intensified with expanded invasion forces occupying the Donbas. [LINK: ([213]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-218]  On 22 May, the BBC reported that after the fall of Mariupol, Russia had intensified offensives in Luhansk and Donetsk while concentrating missile attacks and intense artillery fire on Sievierodonetsk, the largest city under Ukrainian control in Luhansk Oblast. [LINK: ([214]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-219]  On 23 May, Russian forces were reported entering  [LINK: (Lyman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyman,_Ukraine] , capturing the city by 26 May. [LINK: ([215]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-220]  [LINK: ([216]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-221]  Ukrainian forces were reported leaving  [LINK: (Sviatohirsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sviatohirsk] . [LINK: ([217]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RID_Rivista_Italiana_Difesa-222]  By 24 May, Russian forces captured  [LINK: (Svitlodarsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Svitlodarsk] . [LINK: ([218]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Svitlodarsk24522-223]  On 30 May, Reuters reported that Russian troops had breached the outskirts of Sievierodonetsk. [LINK: ([219]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-224]  By 2 June, The Washington Post reported that Sievierodonetsk was on the brink of capitulation with over 80% in the hands of Russian troops. [LINK: ([220]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-225]  On 3 June, Ukrainian forces reportedly began a counter-attack in Sievierodonetsk. By 4 June, Ukrainian government sources claimed 20% or more of the city had been recaptured. [LINK: ([221]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-226]

Ukrainian authorities estimated that 800 Ukrainian civilians were besieged at the  [LINK: (Azot) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azot_(Sievierodonetsk)]  chemical factory in Sievierodonetsk; Russian-backed separatists said it sheltered 300–400 soldiers. [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-227]  [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-228]  With the Ukrainian defences of Sievierodonetsk faltering, Russian troops began intensifying their attack on the neighbouring city of  [LINK: (Lysychansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lysychansk] . [LINK: ([224]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-229]  On 20 June it was reported that Russian troops continued to tighten their grip on Sievierodonetsk by capturing surrounding villages. [LINK: ([225]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-230]

On 24 June,  [LINK: (CNN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNN]  reported that, amid scorched-earth tactics by advancing Russian troops, Ukraine's armed forces were ordered to evacuate Sievierodonetsk; several hundred civilians taking refuge in the Azot chemical plant were left behind, with some comparing their plight to those in Mariupol. [LINK: ([226]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-231]  On 3 July, the Russian defence ministry claimed Lysychansk had been captured and occupied. [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-232]  On 4 July, The Guardian reported that after the fall of the Luhansk oblast, Russian troops would continue their invasion into the adjacent  [LINK: (Donetsk Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk_Oblast]  to attack the cities of Sloviansk and  [LINK: (Bakhmut) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bakhmut] . [LINK: ([228]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-233]

Russian forces continued to fire missiles and drop bombs on  [LINK: (Dnipro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnipro]  and  [LINK: (Zaporizhzhia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaporizhzhia] . [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nytimes.com-189]  Russian missiles destroyed the  [LINK: (Dnipro International Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnipro_International_Airport]  on 10 April. [LINK: ([229]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-234]  On 2 May, the UN, reportedly with the cooperation of Russian troops, evacuated about 100 survivors from the siege of Mariupol. [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-235]  On 28 June, Reuters reported that a Russian missile attack on  [LINK: (Kremenchuk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kremenchuk] , detonated in a mall, had killed at least 18. France's Macron called it a "war crime". [LINK: ([231]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-236]

The president of Ukrainian nuclear agency  [LINK: (Energoatom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energoatom]  called the situation at the Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant "extremely tense", saying that it was being operated by Ukrainian staff but controlled by up to 500 Russian soldiers, with Russia shelling nearby areas and storing weapons at the plant. [LINK: ([232]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-237]  Russia agreed on 19 August to allow IAEA inspectors access to the plant after a call from Macron to Putin. As of July 2023, access to the plant remained limited. [LINK: ([233]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-238]

Russia reported that 12 attacks with explosions from 50 artillery shells had been recorded by 18 August at the plant and the company town of  [LINK: (Enerhodar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enerhodar] . [LINK: ([234]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nei-20220822-239]   [LINK: (Tobias Ellwood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobias_Ellwood] , chair of the UK's  [LINK: (Defence Select Committee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defence_Select_Committee] , and US congressman  [LINK: (Adam Kinzinger) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_Kinzinger]  said that radiation leaks would be a breach of the  [LINK: (North Atlantic Treaty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Atlantic_Treaty] , under which an attack on a member of NATO is an attack on them all. [LINK: ([235]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-240]  [LINK: ([236]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-241]

On 6 September 2022, Ukrainian forces launched a surprise  [LINK: (counteroffensive in the Kharkiv region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Kharkiv_counteroffensive] , beginning near  [LINK: (Balakliia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balakliia] , led by  [LINK: (General Syrskyi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oleksandr_Syrskyi] . [LINK: ([237]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RO-242]  An emboldened Kyiv launched a counteroffensive 12 September around Kharkiv successful enough to make Russia admit losing key positions and for The New York Times to say that it dented the image of a "Mighty Putin". Kyiv sought more arms from the West to sustain the counteroffensive. [LINK: ([238]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-243]  On 21 September 2022, Vladimir Putin announced  [LINK: (a partial mobilisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Russian_mobilization]  and Minister of Defence  [LINK: (Sergei Shoigu) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Shoigu]  said 300,000 reservists would be called. [LINK: ([239]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-France_24_2022-244]  He also said that his country would use "all means" to "defend itself". Mykhailo Podolyak, an adviser to Zelenskyy, said that the decision was predictable and that it was an attempt to justify "Russia's failures". [LINK: ([240]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-gazetapl_2022_pd-245]  British Foreign Office Minister Gillian Keegan called the situation an "escalation", [LINK: ([241]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Ellyatt_2022_esc-246]  while former Mongolian president  [LINK: (Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsakhiagiin_Elbegdorj]  accused Russia of using Russian Mongols as "cannon fodder". [LINK: ([242]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-247]

In late September 2022, Russian-installed officials in Ukraine organised  [LINK: (referendums) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_annexation_referendums_in_Russian-occupied_Ukraine]  on the annexation of the Donetsk People's Republic and the Luhansk People's Republic, as well as the Russian-appointed military administrations of Kherson Oblast and Zaporizhzhia Oblast. Denounced by Ukraine's government and allies, including the UK, US, and France, as  [LINK: (sham elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sham_election] , official results showed overwhelming majorities in favour of annexation. [LINK: ([243]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-248]

On 30 September 2022, Vladimir Putin announced the annexation of Ukraine's Donetsk, Luhansk, Kherson and Zaporizhzhia oblasts in an address to the  [LINK: (Russian parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Assembly_(Russia)] . [LINK: ([244]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-249]  Ukraine, the United States, the European Union and the United Nations all denounced the annexation as illegal. [LINK: ([245]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-250]

On 29 August, Zelenskyy announced the start of a full-scale counteroffensive in the southeast to retake Russian-occupied territory in the Kherson and Mykolaiv regions. [LINK: ([246]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-251]  By 4 September, Ukrainian forces had retaken the village of  [LINK: (Vysokopillia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vysokopillia] . [LINK: ([247]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-252]  Ukrainian attacks also continued along the southern frontline, though reports about territorial changes were largely unverifiable. [LINK: ([248]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-253]

In October, Ukrainian forces pushed further south towards the city of Kherson, taking control of 1,170 square kilometres (450 sq mi), with fighting extending to  [LINK: (Dudchany) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dudchany] . [LINK: ([249]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-254]  [LINK: ([250]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-255]  On 9 November, defence minister Shoigu ordered Russian forces to leave part of Kherson Oblast, including the city of  [LINK: (Kherson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_of_Kherson] , and move to the eastern bank of the Dnieper. [LINK: ([251]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-256]  On 11 November, Ukrainian troops entered Kherson, as Russia completed its withdrawal. This meant that Russian forces no longer had a foothold on the west bank of the Dnieper. [LINK: ([252]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-257]

Ukrainian forces launched another surprise counteroffensive on 6 September in the Kharkiv Oblast near  [LINK: (Balakliia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balakliia]  led by General Syrskyi. [LINK: ([237]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-RO-242]  By 7 September, Ukrainian forces had advanced 20 kilometres (12 mi) into Russian-occupied territory and claimed to have recaptured approximately 400 square kilometres (150 sq mi). Russian commentators said this was likely due to the relocation of Russian forces to Kherson. [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-258]  On 8 September, Ukrainian forces captured Balakliia and advanced to within 15 kilometres (9.3 mi) of  [LINK: (Kupiansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kupiansk] . [LINK: ([254]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-259]  Military analysts said Ukrainian forces appeared to be moving towards Kupiansk, a major railway hub, with the aim of cutting off the Russian forces at Izium from the north. [LINK: ([255]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-260]

On 9 September, the  [LINK: (Russian occupation administration of Kharkiv Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_occupation_of_Kharkiv_Oblast]  announced it would "evacuate" the civilian populations of Izium, Kupiansk and  [LINK: (Velykyi Burluk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velykyi_Burluk] . The  [LINK: (Institute for the Study of War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_for_the_Study_of_War]  (ISW) said it believed Kupiansk would likely fall in the next 72 hours, [LINK: ([256]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-261]  while Russian reserve units were sent to the area by both road and helicopter. [LINK: ([257]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-262]  On the morning of 10 September, photos emerged claiming to depict Ukrainian troops raising the Ukrainian flag in the centre of Kupiansk, [LINK: ([258]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-kupiansk-euromaidanpress-263]  and the ISW said Ukrainian forces had captured approximately 2,500 square kilometres (970 sq mi). [LINK: ([259]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-isw20220909-264]  Reuters reported that Russian positions in northeast Ukraine had "collapsed" in the face of the Ukrainian assault, with Russian forces forced to withdraw from their base at Izium after being cut off by the capture of Kupiansk. [LINK: ([260]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-265]

By 15 September, an assessment by the  [LINK: (UK Ministry of Defence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Defence_(United_Kingdom)]  confirmed that Russia had either lost or withdrawn from almost all of their positions west of the  [LINK: (Oskil river) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oskil_(river)] , abandoning high-value military assets. [LINK: ([261]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-266]  The offensive continued pushing east and by 1 October, Ukrainian forces  [LINK: (had liberated Lyman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_battle_of_Lyman] . [LINK: ([262]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-267]

After the end of the twin Ukrainian counteroffensives, the fighting semi-deadlocked during the winter, [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TakesStock-268]  with heavy casualties but reduced motion of the frontline. [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-269]  Russia launched a self-proclaimed winter offensive in eastern Ukraine, but the campaign stalled with limited gains. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TakesStock-268]  [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-aground-270]  Analysts blamed the failure on Russia's lack of "trained men" and supply problems with artillery ammunition, among other problems. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TakesStock-268]  [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-aground-270]  Near the end of May,  [LINK: (Mark Galeotti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Galeotti]  assessed that "after Russia's abortive and ill-conceived winter offensive, which squandered its opportunity to consolidate its forces, Ukraine is in a relatively strong position." [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-271]

On 7 February, The New York Times reported that Russians had newly mobilised nearly 200,000 soldiers to participate in the offensive in the Donbas. [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-272]  The Russian private military company Wagner Group took on greater prominence in the war, [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-273]  leading "grinding advances" in  [LINK: (Bakhmut) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bakhmut]  with tens of thousands of recruits from  [LINK: (prison battalions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penal_military_unit]  taking part in "near suicidal" assaults on Ukrainian positions. [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-aground-270]

In late January 2023, fighting intensified in southern Zaporizhzhia Oblast, with both sides suffering heavy casualties. [LINK: ([269]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-274]  In nearby southern parts of Donetsk Oblast, an  [LINK: (intense, three-week Russian assault) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Vuhledar]  near the coal-mining town of  [LINK: (Vuhledar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vuhledar]  was called the largest tank battle of the war to date, and ended in disaster for Russian forces, who lost "at least 130 tanks and armored personnel carriers" according to Ukrainian commanders. The  [LINK: (British Ministry of Defence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Ministry_of_Defence]  stated that "a whole Russian brigade was effectively annihilated." [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-275]  [LINK: ([271]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-TankKramer-276]

In late 2022, as Russian casualties exceeded 50,000, the Russian army introduced  [LINK: (barrier troops) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrier_troops] . The UK defence ministry stated that these are units that threaten to shoot their own retreating soldiers to compel offensives. In March 2023, Russian soldiers filmed a video addressed to Putin where they stated that after suffering casualties, they attempted to return to their headquarters but their superiors denied them evacuation and barrier troops were placed behind them threatening to "destroy them". [LINK: ([272]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Sauer_2023_a785-277]  In particular,  [LINK: (Storm-Z) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storm-Z]  units have been reported to be "kept in line" by barrier troops. [LINK: ([273]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Axe_2024_h250-278]

Following defeat in Kherson and Kharkiv, Russian and Wagner forces focused on taking Bakhmut and breaking the half-year-long stalemate there since the start of the war. Russian forces sought to encircle the city, attacking from the north via  [LINK: (Soledar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soledar] . After taking heavy casualties, Russian and Wagner forces  [LINK: (took control of Soledar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Soledar]  on 16 January 2023. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-279]  [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-280]  By early February 2023, Bakhmut was facing attacks from north, south and east, with the sole Ukrainian supply lines coming from  [LINK: (Chasiv Yar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chasiv_Yar]  to the west. [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-281]

On 4 March, Bakhmut's deputy mayor told news services that there was street fighting in the city. [LINK: ([277]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-282]  On 20 May 2023, the Wagner Group claimed full control over Bakhmut, and a victory in the battle was officially declared by Russia the next day, [LINK: ([278]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-283]  following which Wagner forces retreated from the city in place of regular Russian units. [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-284]

In June 2023, Ukrainian forces launched counteroffensives on multiple fronts. [LINK: ([280]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-285]  [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-286]  Efforts faced stiff Russian resistance. [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-287]  By 12 June, Ukraine reported advances and liberated settlements. [LINK: ([283]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-288]  On 24 June, the  [LINK: (Wagner Group rebellion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagner_Group_rebellion]  briefly unfolded before a peace deal. [LINK: ([284]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-289]  In late June, Ukraine reclaimed territory in Donbas and made gains in Kherson Oblast. [LINK: ([285]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-290] 
Russia heavily mined areas, making Ukraine the most mined country in the world. [LINK: ([286]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-291]  Following Russia leaving the Black Sea Grain Initiative, Ukraine targeted Russian ships. [LINK: ([287]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-292]  In September 2023, Russian strikes hit Ukrainian energy facilities. [LINK: ([288]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-293]  The US announced sending long-range ATACMS missiles [LINK: ([289]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-294]  and Ukraine struck the Sevastopol naval base. [LINK: ([290]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-295]  [LINK: ([291]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-296]

In October–December 2023, Ukrainian forces crossed the  [LINK: (Dnipro River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnipro_River]  despite heavy losses. On 1 December 2023, Zelenskyy stated that the Ukrainian counter-offensive was unsuccessful. [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-failedTelegraph-297]  Zelenskyy stated it would be easier to regain Crimea than the Donbas, because the latter is heavily militarised and has much pro-Russian sentiment. [LINK: ([293]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-298]  In December 2023, media outlets described the Ukrainian counteroffensive as having failed to regain significant territory or meet any of its objectives. [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-failedTelegraph-297]  [LINK: ([294]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-failedNYT-299]  [LINK: ([295]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-failedWPT1-300]

In October 2023, it was reported that there were growing mutinies among Russian troops due to the extensive losses in Russian offensives around  [LINK: (Avdiivka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avdiivka] , with a lack of artillery, food, water and poor command also being reported. [LINK: ([296]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Cole_2023_r879-301]  By November, British intelligence said that recent weeks had "likely seen some of the highest Russian casualty rates of the war so far." [LINK: ([297]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-France_24_2023_a290-302]

On 17 February 2024,  [LINK: (Russia captured Avdiivka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Avdiivka_(2023%E2%80%932024)] , a longtime stronghold for Ukraine that had been described as a "gateway" to nearby  [LINK: (Donetsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donetsk] . [LINK: ([298]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Avdiivka_BBCW-303]  [LINK: ([299]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Avdiivka_TGW-304]  [LINK: ([300]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-17Feb24NYT-305]  Described by Forbes journalist David Axe as a pyrrhic Russian victory, the Russian 2nd and 41st Combined Arms Armies ended up with 16,000 men killed, tens of thousands wounded and around 700 vehicles lost. [LINK: ([301]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Axe_2024_u665-306]

Ukraine's shortage of ammunition caused by political deadlock in the US Congress and a lack of production capacity in Europe contributed to the Ukrainian withdrawal from Avdiivka, and was "being felt across the front" according to  [LINK: (Time) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_(magazine)] . The shortage resulted in Ukraine having to ration its units to fire only 2,000 rounds per day, compared to an estimated 10,000 rounds fired daily by Russia. [LINK: ([302]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Watling_2024_x516-307]

On 10 May 2024, Russia began a  [LINK: (renewed offensive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Kharkiv_front_of_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War_(2024%E2%80%93present)]  in  [LINK: (Kharkiv Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kharkiv_Oblast] . Russia captured a dozen villages; Ukraine had evacuated more than 11,000 people from the region since the start of the offensive by 25 May. Ukraine said on 17 May that its forces had slowed the Russian advance, and by 25 May Zelenskyy said that Ukrainian forces had secured "combat control" of areas where Russian troops entered the northeastern Kharkiv Oblast. Russian officials said that they were "advancing in every direction" and that the goal was to create a "buffer zone" for embattled border regions. [LINK: ([303]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-c973-308]  [LINK: ([304]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k589-309]  The White House said on 7 June that the offensive had stalled and was unlikely to advance further. [LINK: ([305]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k069-310]

Following the battle of Avdiivka, Russian forces advanced northwest of it to form a salient,  [LINK: (capturing Ocheretyne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ocheretyne]  in late April [LINK: ([306]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-311]  [LINK: ([307]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_2024-05-04-312]  and further expanding the salient in the succeeding months. [LINK: ([308]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-313]  Russian forces also launched an  [LINK: (offensive towards Chasiv Yar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Chasiv_Yar]  in early April, [LINK: ([309]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-314]  and by early July had captured its easternmost district. [LINK: ([310]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-315]  [LINK: ([311]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-316]  Another  [LINK: (offensive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Toretsk]  in the direction of  [LINK: (Toretsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toretsk]  was launched on 18 June, [LINK: ([312]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_2024-07-18-317]  with the goal of capturing the city, [LINK: ([313]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-318]  and according to Ukrainian military observer and spokesperson Nazar Voloshyn, flanking Chasiv Yar from the south. [LINK: ([314]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_2024-06-21-319]  Russian forces advanced to expand the salient northwest of Avdiivka in July, and on 19 July 2024, made a breakthrough allowing them to  [LINK: (advance towards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pokrovsk_offensive]  the operationally significant city of  [LINK: (Pokrovsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pokrovsk] . [LINK: ([315]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-320]  [LINK: ([316]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_2024-07-19-321]

On 6 August 2024, Ukraine launched their first direct offensive into Russian territory, the largest of any pro-Ukrainian incursion since the invasion's inception, into the bordering  [LINK: (Kursk Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kursk_Oblast] . [LINK: ([317]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-322]  The main axis of the initial advance centred in the direction of  [LINK: (Sudzha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sudzha] , 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) from the border, [LINK: ([318]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-323]  which was reported by President Zelenskyy to have been captured on 15 August. [LINK: ([319]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-324]  Ukraine, taking advantage of the lack of experienced units and defences along the border with Kursk Oblast, was able to quickly seize territory in the opening days of the incursion. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-325]  The incursion caused Russia to divert thousands of troops from occupied Ukrainian territory to counter the threat, [LINK: ([321]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k052-326]  [LINK: ([322]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-wapo831-327]  though not from Donetsk Oblast. [LINK: ([322]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-wapo831-327]

Despite repeated deadlines set by Putin to push out Ukrainian troops, Russian forces had not done so by the end of January 2025, with advances in Donetsk Oblast being prioritised over the Kursk salient. However, by February 2025, Russian forces in Donetsk Oblast were described by the ISW as not being fully protected from the theatre-wide impacts of the incursion, with troops, armoured vehicles, artillery, and air defence systems being pulled away from Ukrainian sectors to reinforce the Russian forces in Kursk. [LINK: ([323]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-y851-328]  [LINK: ([324]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-g362-329]

By the summer of 2025, Ukrainian forces had been mostly repelled from the area. [LINK: ([325]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-330]

Russian troops continued advancing in eastern Ukraine at a faster pace than prior to the Kursk offensive, [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Miller-332]  including towards the operationally important city of  [LINK: (Pokrovsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pokrovsk] , where their forces had instead been increased. [LINK: ([328]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-333]  [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-334]  [LINK: ([330]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-335]

In late August 2024, Russian forces seized  [LINK: (Novohrodivka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novohrodivka] , southeast of Pokrovsk, bringing them within 8 kilometres of the city, [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Miller-332]  while  [LINK: (capturing Krasnohorivka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Krasnohorivka]  [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_2024-09-10-336]  and  [LINK: (Ukrainsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukrainsk]  [LINK: ([332]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-NYT918-337]  in early September. [LINK: ([332]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-NYT918-337]  In late September, a Russian assault on the long-held city of  [LINK: (Vuhledar began) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Vuhledar] ; [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-338]  after it fell on 1 October, the city with a pre-war population of about 14,000 was described as a "sprawling ruin". [LINK: ([334]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-vuhledar101-339]  [LINK: ([335]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-j026-340]

On 30 October, Ukrainian Major General  [LINK: (Dmytro Marchenko) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmytro_Marchenko]  was reported to have said "our front has crumbled" due to a dwindling ammunition supply, problems with military recruitment, and poor leadership. He said  [LINK: (Zelensky's victory plan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victory_Plan_for_Ukraine]  was too heavily focused on Western support. Briefings from Western officials had also become more pessimistic about Ukraine's military situation. [LINK: ([336]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-telegraph-20241030-341]  [LINK: ([337]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nyt-20241101-342]  According to the Prosecutor General's Office, more than 100,000 criminal cases for  [LINK: (desertion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desertion]  were initiated by the end of November 2024. [LINK: ([338]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-343]  Russian forces then captured the city of  [LINK: (Kurakhove) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurakhove]  in late December 2024, and the nearby  [LINK: (Kurakhove Power Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurakhove_Power_Station]  in January 2025. [LINK: ([339]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-344]  [LINK: ([340]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_2025-01-03-345]

In early 2025, Russian forces advanced in eastern Ukraine, [LINK: ([341]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-346]  [LINK: ([342]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-347]  with Russia claiming to have captured  [LINK: (Velyka Novosilka) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velyka_Novosilka]  in January. [LINK: ([343]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-348]  [LINK: ([344]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-349]  Russia continued  [LINK: (attacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_strikes_against_Ukrainian_infrastructure_(2022%E2%80%93present)]  on Ukrainian energy infrastructure. [LINK: ([345]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-350]  In February 2025, Ukraine's government approved a one-year contract for volunteers aged 18–24 not subject to  [LINK: (mobilisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobilisation] . [LINK: ([346]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-351]  By April 2025, Russian advances had slowed, according to Seth Jones from the  [LINK: (Center for Strategic and International Studies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Center_for_Strategic_and_International_Studies] . [LINK: ([347]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-h119-352]  Russian attacks on civilians also intensified, with the month of June seeing the highest civilian casualty count in Ukraine since the start of the invasion, with 232 killed and 1,343 injured. [LINK: ([348]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-o749-353]  Russia killed at least 21 civilians including children in the second largest strike of the war overnight on 27 and 28 August, damaging European diplomatic facilities along with civilian infrastructure. [LINK: ([349]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-c384-354]

Putin declared in March 2025 that "there are reasons to believe we can finish off Ukrainian forces", as a Russian summer offensive was expected. Russia planned to expand into Sumy and Kharkiv, however, the actual offensive was generally considered a failure with modest gains at the cost of heavy casualties. By September 2025, Russian forces had still not achieved any frontline breakthroughs, with key strategic objectives like Pokrovsk still held by Ukraine. [LINK: ([350]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-b037-355]  [LINK: ([351]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-x018-356]

During October and November 2025, Russian forces intensified operations around Pokrovsk. According to open-source military assessments, Russia advanced hundreds of square miles during this period, applying sustained pressure on Ukrainian defensive lines. [LINK: ([352]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-357]  Zelenskyy stated that Russia had deployed approximately 170,000 troops in response, describing the situation as difficult but highlighting continued Ukrainian resistance. [LINK: ([353]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-358]  [LINK: ([354]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-359]  By late October, there was fighting within Pokrovsk city proper. [LINK: ([355]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-360]  On 1 December, Russia claimed to have captured the city, although there were still Ukrainian forces present in the northern portion of the city. [LINK: ([356]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-361]  [LINK: ([357]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-362]

In December 2025, following Russian claims of controlling Kupiansk, Zelenskyy visited the city and praised the troops defending it. Ukraine said it had retaken parts of Kupiansk, and a Ukrainian commander stated that Russian troops stationed there were completely surrounded. [LINK: ([358]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-y195-363]  [LINK: ([359]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-364]  The ISW reported that Russian ultranationalist milbloggers acknowledged the severity of the situation for Russian forces, and that Russian claims of advances in the area had been used as part of a  [LINK: (cognitive warfare) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_warfare]  effort. [LINK: ([360]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-d830-365]

Russia also escalated attacks on Ukrainian energy infrastructure throughout the period. [LINK: ([361]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Chathamhouse_Nov2025-366]  In one major strike, over 450 drones and 45 missiles were reportedly used, according to Ukrainian officials, causing civilian casualties and damaging critical infrastructure. In response, Ukraine attacked Russian energy and logistical networks, including targeting oil facilities, causing a Russian  [LINK: (fuel crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Russian_fuel_crisis] . [LINK: ([362]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-367]  [LINK: ([363]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-368]  [LINK: ([364]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-o817-369]


# BATTLESPACES #

The supreme commanders-in-chief are the heads of state: President Putin of Russia and President Zelenskyy of Ukraine. Putin has reportedly meddled in operational decisions, bypassing senior commanders and giving orders directly to brigade commanders. [LINK: ([365]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-370]

US general  [LINK: (Mark Milley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Milley]  wrote in May 2022 that Ukraine's top military commander in the war,  [LINK: (commander-in-chief of the Armed Forces of Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commander-in-Chief_of_the_Armed_Forces_of_Ukraine] , General  [LINK: (Valerii Zaluzhnyi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valerii_Zaluzhnyi] , "has emerged as the military mind his country needed. His leadership enabled the Ukrainian armed forces to adapt quickly with battlefield initiative against the Russians." [LINK: ([366]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Shuster_2022-371]  Russia began the invasion with no overall commander. The commanders of the four military districts were each responsible for their own offensives. [LINK: ([367]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CRSR21323-372]

After initial setbacks, the commander of the Russian Southern Military District,  [LINK: (Aleksandr Dvornikov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleksandr_Dvornikov] , was placed in overall command on 8 April 2022, [LINK: ([368]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-373]  while still responsible for his own campaign. Russian forces benefited from the centralisation of command under Dvornikov, [LINK: ([369]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-374]  but continued failures to meet expectations in Moscow led to multiple changes in overall command: [LINK: ([367]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CRSR21323-372]

commander of the Eastern Military District  [LINK: (Gennady Zhidko) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gennady_Zhidko]  (Eastern Military District, 26  – 8 May October 2022)

commander of the southern grouping of forces  [LINK: (Sergei Surovikin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Surovikin]  (early October 2022 – 11 January 2023)

commander-in-chief of the Russian Armed Forces  [LINK: (Valerii Gerasimov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valerii_Gerasimov]  (from 11 January 2023)

Russia has suffered a remarkably large number of casualties in the ranks of its officers, including  [LINK: (a number of generals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Russian_generals_killed_during_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] . [LINK: ([370]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-375]

Aerial warfare began the first day of the invasion. Dozens of missile attacks were recorded across both eastern and western Ukraine, [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CriticalThreats-129]  [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ISW_4MAR-130]  as far west as Lviv. [LINK: ([371]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Rostov-Gazeta-2022-02-25-376]

By September 2022, Ukrainian air forces had shot down about 55 Russian warplanes. [LINK: ([372]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-377]  In mid-October, Russian forces launched  [LINK: (missile strikes against Ukrainian infrastructure) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_strikes_against_Ukrainian_infrastructure] , intended to knock out energy facilities. [LINK: ([373]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-378]  By late November, hundreds of civilians had been killed or wounded, [LINK: ([374]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-379]  and  [LINK: (rolling blackouts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling_blackout]  had left millions without power. [LINK: ([375]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-380]

In December 2022, drones launched from Ukraine allegedly carried out several attacks on  [LINK: (Dyagilevo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyagilevo_(air_base)]  and  [LINK: (Engels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engels-2_(air_base)]  air bases in  [LINK: (western Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022%E2%80%932023_Western_Russia_attacks] , killing 10 and heavily damaging two  [LINK: (Tu-95) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tupolev_Tu-95]  aircraft. [LINK: ([376]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-381]

On 1 June 2025, Ukraine carried out " [LINK: (Operation Spiderweb) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Spiderweb] ", targeting several air bases deep inside Russian territory using smuggled drones. The operation was seen as successful, [LINK: ([377]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-z595-382]  with Ukraine saying it had damaged or destroyed 41 high-value aircraft, including strategic bombers used to coordinate and launch attacks on Ukrainian cities. [LINK: ([378]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-b319-383]  US officials assessed a lower number, saying that Ukraine had hit 20 planes, destroying 10. [LINK: ([379]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-s394-384]  The operation is estimated to have caused billions of dollars in damage to Russian aircraft. [LINK: ([380]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-b759-385]  [LINK: ([381]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-p895-386]  [LINK: ([382]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-t713-387]  [LINK: ([383]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-d975-388]

On 31 July 2022, Russian Navy Day commemorations were cancelled after a drone attack reportedly wounded several people at the Russian  [LINK: (Black Sea Fleet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sea_Fleet]  headquarters in Sevastopol. [LINK: ([384]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-389]  On 9 August 2022,  [LINK: (large explosions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Novofedorivka_explosions]  were reported at  [LINK: (Saky Air Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saky_(air_base)]  in western Crimea. Satellite imagery showed at least eight aircraft damaged or destroyed. Initial speculation attributed the explosions to long-range missiles, sabotage by special forces or an accident; [LINK: ([385]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-390]  Ukrainian general  [LINK: (Valerii Zaluzhnyi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valerii_Zaluzhnyi]  claimed responsibility on 7 September. [LINK: ([386]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-391]

A week later Russia blamed "sabotage" for explosions and a fire at an arms depot near  [LINK: (Dzhankoi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzhankoi]  in northeastern Crimea that also damaged a railway line and power station. Russian regional head  [LINK: (Sergei Aksyonov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Aksyonov]  said that 2,000 people were evacuated from the area. [LINK: ([387]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-392]  On 18 August, explosions were reported at  [LINK: (Belbek Air Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belbek_Air_Base]  north of Sevastopol. [LINK: ([388]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-393]  On 8 October 2022 the  [LINK: (Kerch Bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerch_Bridge] , linking occupied Crimea to Russia,  [LINK: (partially collapsed due to an explosion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Crimean_Bridge_explosion] . [LINK: ([389]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nyttruck-394]  On 17 July 2023, there was  [LINK: (another large explosion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Crimean_Bridge_explosion]  on the bridge. [LINK: ([390]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-395]  On 3 June 2025, an  [LINK: (attack with underwater explosives) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Crimean_Bridge_explosion]  damaged the foundations of the bridge; traffic resumed within hours. [LINK: ([391]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-396]

Since 2022, Russia has carried out strikes on Ukrainian electrical and water systems. [LINK: ([392]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-intermittent-397]  On 6 October 2022 the Ukrainian military reported that 86  [LINK: (Shahed 136) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HESA_Shahed_136]  kamikaze drones had been launched by Russian forces, and between 30 September and 6 October Ukrainian forces had destroyed 24 out of 46 launched. [LINK: ([393]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nw1-398]  On 8 October, it was announced that  [LINK: (General of the Army) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_of_the_Army]   [LINK: (Sergey Surovikin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergey_Surovikin]  would be commanding all Russian forces on the strength of his novel air assault technique. [LINK: ([394]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-alar1-399]  On 16 October,  [LINK: (The Washington Post) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Washington_Post]  reported that Iran was planning to supply Russia with drones and missiles. [LINK: ([395]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-400]  On 18 October the US State Department accused Iran of violating  [LINK: (Resolution 2231) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_2231]  by selling  [LINK: (Shahed 131) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahed_131]  and  [LINK: (Shahed 136) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahed_136]  drones to Russia, [LINK: ([396]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-401]  agreeing with France and the UK. Iran denied sending arms for the Ukraine war. [LINK: ([397]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CNN:_Iran_denies_supplying_Russia_with_weapons_for_use_in_Ukraine-402]  On 22 October France, Britain and Germany called for a UN investigation. [LINK: ([398]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-f24-20221022-403]  On 1 November, CNN reported that Iran was preparing to send ballistic missiles and other weapons to Russia for use in Ukraine. [LINK: ([399]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-404]

On 15 November 2022, Russia fired 85 missiles at the  [LINK: (Ukrainian power grid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity_in_Ukraine] , causing  [LINK: (power outages) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_outage]  in Kyiv and neighbouring regions. [LINK: ([400]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-405]  In March 2023, The New York Times reported that Russia had used new hypersonic missiles in a massive missile attack on Ukraine. Such missiles are more effective in evading conventional Ukrainian anti-missile defences. [LINK: ([401]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nyt2-406]

The UK Defence Ministry said strikes against Ukrainian infrastructure are part of Russia's 'Strategic Operation for the Destruction of Critically Important Targets' (SODCIT) military doctrine, intended to demoralise the population and force the Ukrainian leadership to capitulate. [LINK: ([402]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-407]  According to the  [LINK: (Royal United Services Institute) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_United_Services_Institute] : [LINK: ([403]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-408]

Russian strikes had cumulatively destroyed 9  [LINK: (gigawatts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigawatts]  (GW) of Ukraine's domestic power generation by mid-June 2024. Peak consumption during the winter of 2023 was 18 GW, which means that half of Ukraine's production capacity has been destroyed.

On 8 July 2024, Russia used a  [LINK: (Kh-101) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kh-101]  missile [LINK: ([404]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-bc8-409]  to kill two and injure 16 at the  [LINK: (Okhmatdyt Children's Hospital) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okhmatdyt]  in Kyiv. [LINK: ([405]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ap8-410]  [LINK: ([406]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-cnn8-411]  [LINK: ([407]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-bbc8-412]  [LINK: ([408]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-cbc8-413]  At least 20 civilians were killed in Kyiv that night. [LINK: ([409]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-peu8-414]

In late 2024, Russia switched from attacking energy distribution infrastructure to striking power stations, affecting 15% of Ukraine's pre-war generating capacity. From the summer of 2025, attacks switched back to infrastructure with a much greater number of drones and ballistic missiles. Strikes were also made on Ukrainian gas production, with 60% of its capacity destroyed in October 2025. [LINK: ([361]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Chathamhouse_Nov2025-366]

In 2025, Ukraine intensified efforts to disrupt Russian oil production and export facilities, using drone and missile strikes assisted by Western targeting intelligence. By late October 2025, an estimated 50% of Russia's 38 major refineries had been hit more than once. The result has been an estimated drop in oil production of between 10 and 15%, leading to raised domestic fuel prices and shortages in some regions. [LINK: ([361]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Chathamhouse_Nov2025-366]

On 28 February 2022, Turkey invoked the 1936  [LINK: (Montreux Convention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montreux_Convention]  and sealed off the  [LINK: (Bosphorus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bosphorus]  and  [LINK: (Dardanelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dardanelles]  straits to Russian warships that were not registered to  [LINK: (Black Sea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sea]  home bases and returning to their ports of origin. It specifically denied passage to four Russian naval vessels. [LINK: ([410]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-415]  On 24 February, the  [LINK: (State Border Guard Service of Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Border_Guard_Service_of_Ukraine]  announced that Russian Navy ships had begun an  [LINK: (attack on Snake Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_Island_campaign] . [LINK: ([411]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-416]  The guided missile cruiser and flagship of the  [LINK: (Black Sea Fleet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sea_Fleet] ,  [LINK: (Moskva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_cruiser_Moskva]  and patrol boat  [LINK: (Vasily Bykov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_patrol_ship_Vasily_Bykov]  bombarded the island. [LINK: ([412]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-417]  The Russian warship instructed the Ukrainians on the island to surrender. Their response was " [LINK: (Russian warship, go fuck yourself!) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_warship,_go_fuck_yourself] " [LINK: ([413]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-418]  After the bombardment, Russian soldiers landed and took control of  [LINK: (Snake Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_Island_(Ukraine)] . [LINK: ([414]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-419]  Russia said on 26 February that US drones had supplied intelligence to the Ukrainian navy to help it target Russian warships in the Black Sea. The US denied this. [LINK: ([415]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-420]

By 3 March, Ukrainian forces in Mykolaiv  [LINK: (scuttled) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scuttled]  the frigate  [LINK: (Hetman Sahaidachny) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukrainian_frigate_Hetman_Sahaidachny] , the flagship of the Ukrainian navy, to prevent its capture. [LINK: ([416]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-421]  On 24 March, Ukrainian officials said that a Russian  [LINK: (landing ship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alligator-class_landing_ship]  docked in Berdiansk was destroyed by a Ukrainian rocket attack. [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CNN-Berdyansk-172]  [LINK: ([417]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-422]  In March 2022, the UN  [LINK: (International Maritime Organization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Maritime_Organization]  (IMO) sought to create a safe sea corridor for commercial vessels to leave Ukrainian ports. [LINK: ([418]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-reuters-20220312-423]  On 27 March, Russia established a sea corridor 80 miles (130 km) long and 3 miles (4.8 km) wide through its  [LINK: (Maritime Exclusion Zone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maritime_Exclusion_Zone] , for the transit of merchant vessels from the edge of Ukrainian territorial waters southeast of Odesa. [LINK: ([419]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-westpoint-20220412-424]  [LINK: ([420]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-imo-20220328-425]

The Moskva was, according to Ukrainian sources and a US senior official, [LINK: ([421]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-426]  hit on 13 April by two Ukrainian  [LINK: (Neptune anti-ship cruise missiles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R-360_Neptune] . The Russian Defence Ministry said the warship had suffered serious damage from a munition explosion caused by a fire, and that its crew had been evacuated. [LINK: ([422]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-427]  Pentagon spokesman John Kirby reported on 14 April that satellite images showed that the Russian warship had suffered a sizeable explosion onboard but was heading to the east for expected repairs in  [LINK: (Sevastopol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sevastopol] . [LINK: ([423]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-428]  Later the same day, the Russian Ministry of Defence stated that the  [LINK: (Moskva had sunk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_of_the_Moskva] . [LINK: ([424]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-429]  On 15 April, Reuters reported that Russia launched an apparent retaliatory missile strike against the missile factory  [LINK: (Luch Design Bureau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luch_Design_Bureau]  in Kyiv where the Neptune missiles used in the Moskva attack were manufactured. [LINK: ([425]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-430]  On 5 May, a US official confirmed that the US gave "a range of intelligence" to assist in the sinking. [LINK: ([426]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-431]

On 1 June, Russian Foreign Minister  [LINK: (Sergey Lavrov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergey_Lavrov]  asserted that Ukraine's policy of mining its own harbours to impede Russian maritime aggression had contributed to the food export crisis, saying: "If Kyiv solves the problem of demining ports, the Russian Navy will ensure the unimpeded passage of ships with grain to the Mediterranean Sea." [LINK: ([427]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-432]  On 23 July, CNBC reported a Russian missile strike on the Ukrainian port of Odesa, swiftly condemned by world leaders amid a recent  [LINK: (UN- and Turkish-brokered deal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sea_Grain_Initiative]  to secure a sea corridor for exports of foodstuffs. [LINK: ([428]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-433]

On 26 December 2023, Ukraine's air force attacked the  [LINK: (Russian landing ship Novocherkassk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_landing_ship_Novocherkassk]  docked in  [LINK: (Feodosia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feodosia] . Ukraine said it was destroyed. Russian authorities confirmed the attack, but not the loss, and said two attacking aircraft were destroyed. Independent analysts said the ship's loss could hamper future Russian attacks on Ukraine's coast. [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-strikes_2023_12_26_reuters-434]  [LINK: ([430]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-attack_2023_12_26_foxnews-435]  [LINK: ([431]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-strikes_2023_12_26_wsj_com-436]  On 31 January 2024, Ukrainian sea drones struck the Russian  [LINK: (Tarantul-class corvette) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarantul-class_corvette]  Ivanovets in the Black Sea, causing the ship to sink. [LINK: ([432]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-The_Telegraph_2024_e989-437]  [LINK: ([433]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Greenall_2024_j308-438]  On 14 February, the same type of Ukrainian sea drones struck and sank the  [LINK: (Russian landing ship Tsezar Kunikov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_ship_Tsezar_Kunikov] . [LINK: ([434]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Goryashko_2024_x892-439]  [LINK: ([435]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Tom_Balmforth_2024_k016-440]

Ukrainian civilians resisted the Russian invasion by volunteering for territorial defence units, making  [LINK: (Molotov cocktails) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molotov_cocktail] , donating food, building barriers like  [LINK: (Czech hedgehogs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Czech_hedgehog] , [LINK: ([436]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-441]  and helping to transport refugees. [LINK: ([437]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-442]  Responding to a call from  [LINK: (Ukravtodor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Agency_of_Automobile_Roads_of_Ukraine] , Ukraine's transportation agency, civilians dismantled or altered road signs, [LINK: ([438]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-443]  constructed makeshift barriers, and blocked roadways. [LINK: ([439]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-444]  Social media reports showed spontaneous street  [LINK: (protests against Russian forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_protests_in_Russian-occupied_Ukraine]  in occupied settlements, often evolving into verbal altercations and physical standoffs with Russian troops. [LINK: ([440]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-yes!-445]  By April, Ukrainian civilians began to organise as guerrillas, mostly in the wooded north and east of the country. The Ukrainian military announced plans for a large-scale guerrilla campaign to complement its conventional defence. [LINK: ([441]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-446]

People physically blocked Russian military vehicles, sometimes forcing them to retreat. [LINK: ([440]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-yes!-445]  [LINK: ([442]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-447]  The Russian soldiers' response to unarmed civilian resistance varied from reluctance to engage the protesters, [LINK: ([440]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-yes!-445]  to firing directly into crowds. [LINK: ([443]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-448]  There have been mass detentions of Ukrainian protesters, and Ukrainian media has reported forced disappearances, mock executions, hostage-taking,  [LINK: (extrajudicial killings) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extrajudicial_killing] , and sexual violence perpetrated by the Russian military. [LINK: ([444]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-449]  To facilitate Ukrainian attacks, civilians reported Russian military positions via a  [LINK: (Telegram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telegram_(software)]  chatbot and  [LINK: (Diia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diia] , a Ukrainian government app. In response, Russian forces began destroying mobile phone network equipment, searching door-to-door for smartphones and computers, and in at least one case killed a civilian who had pictures of Russian tanks. [LINK: ([445]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-450] 
As of 21 May 2022, Zelenskyy indicated that Ukraine had 700,000 service members on active duty fighting the Russian invasion. [LINK: ([446]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-451]  Ukraine withdrew soldiers and military equipment back to Ukraine over the course of 2022 that had been deployed to  [LINK: (United Nations peacekeeping) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_peacekeeping]  missions. [LINK: ([447]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-452]

Throughout the war Russia and Ukraine have attacked each other's energy infrastructure. Ukrainian power generation and heating facilities and Russia's pipelines and refineries were hit. [LINK: ([448]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-453]  [LINK: ([449]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-gas_infra-454]

As a reaction to  [LINK: (Russia's recognition of the occupied Ukrainian territories in Donetsk and Luhansk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_recognition_of_the_Donetsk_People%27s_Republic_and_the_Luhansk_People%27s_Republic] , Germany suspended its certification of the  [LINK: (Nord Stream 2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nord_Stream_2]  pipeline on 22 February 2022. [LINK: ([450]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-455]  In September 2022 the inactive Nord Stream pipeline between Russia and Germany was  [LINK: (sabotaged) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nord_Stream_pipelines_sabotage] , destroying three of the four pipelines. As of August 2025 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)&action=edit]  no party has claimed nor admitted responsibility and investigations are ongoing. A Ukrainian national was arrested in August 2025 on suspicion of being involved. [LINK: ([451]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-456]  As part of the  [LINK: (economic sanctions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_sanctions]  against Russia for its war the  [LINK: (EU Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EU_Commission]  has banned use of the Nord Stream pipelines. [LINK: ([452]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-457]  [LINK: ([453]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-458]

Pipelines carrying natural gas and oil from Russia to Central Europe continued to operate during the war in a reduced manner. They have been attacked several times; in May 2025 both sides accused each other of attacking gas infrastructure in Russia's Kursk Oblast. In August 2025 Ukraine repeatedly attacked pumping stations of Russia's  [LINK: (Druzhba pipeline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druzhba_pipeline] , interrupting oil supply to Hungary and Slovakia, [LINK: ([449]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-gas_infra-454]  [LINK: ([454]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-459]  while the same month a Russian attack on Ukrainian energy infrastructure left more than 100,000 households without electricity. [LINK: ([455]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-u402-460]

By 28 August 2025 Ukraine's campaign to strike at Russia's petroleum industry had hit ten oil refineries and was estimated by  [LINK: (Reuters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reuters]  to have disrupted Russia's refinery capacity by at least 17% or 1.1 million barrels a day. [LINK: ([456]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-461]  The effect was a fuel crisis in Crimea and both southern and far eastern regions with price surges and dry gas stations. [LINK: ([457]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-462]  In September 2025 the  [LINK: (International Energy Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Energy_Agency]  stated that Russia's revenues from  [LINK: (oil product exports) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petroleum_industry_in_Russia]  had in August declined to five-year lows, contributing to Russia's economic slowdown. [LINK: ([458]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-463]  Acknowledging Russia's strained  [LINK: (energy system) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_in_Russia]  president  [LINK: (Vladimir Putin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Putin]  pointed to Russia's  [LINK: (coal reserves) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coal_in_Russia]  to offset its gas shortage, insufficient infrastructure and under-developed grid economy. [LINK: ([459]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-464]

Following its 14 September 2025 attack on Russia's second largest refinery  [LINK: (Kinef) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinef]  the  [LINK: (General Staff of the Ukrainian Armed Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Staff_of_the_Ukrainian_Armed_Forces]  claimed to be "working 'systematically' to weaken Russia's military and economic capabilities with a particular focus on fuel, weapons, and ammunition production". [LINK: ([460]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-465]


# FOREIGN INVOLVEMENT #

Many countries have supplied military aid to Ukraine including weaponry, equipment, training, and logistical support. Several imposed limited sanctions on Russia in the prelude to the invasion and applied new sanctions when the invasion began, intending to cripple the Russian economy; [LINK: ([461]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DA-466]  [LINK: ([462]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-467]  sanctions targeted individuals, banks, businesses, monetary exchanges, exports, and imports. [LINK: ([461]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DA-466]

Military assistance has mostly been co-ordinated through the  [LINK: (Ukraine Defence Contact Group) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine_Defence_Contact_Group] , whose more than fifty countries include all 32  [LINK: (member states) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Member_states_of_NATO]  of  [LINK: (NATO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO] . [LINK: ([463]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-468]  From January 2022 to January 2024, $380 billion in aid to Ukraine was tracked by the  [LINK: (Kiel Institute) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiel_Institute_for_the_World_Economy] . [LINK: ([464]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-cnn-aid-469]  European countries have provided the most aid in total (military, financial and humanitarian). [LINK: ([465]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CNN_US_investment-470]  Estonia and Denmark have provided the most military aid as a share of GDP. [LINK: ([466]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CFR-471]  The European Union co-ordinated the supply of military aid for the first time. [LINK: ([467]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-472]  The  [LINK: (US has provided) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_and_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  the most military assistance to Ukraine, [LINK: ([465]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CNN_US_investment-470]  and has set aside $175 billion to help the country. [LINK: ([466]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CFR-471]  Most of the US military aid has been old American weaponry and equipment from  [LINK: (reserve stockpiles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_reserve_stock] , while most of the US funding earmarked for Ukraine stays in the US economy and supports US industries, subsidising the manufacture of weapons and military equipment. [LINK: ([466]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CFR-471]  [LINK: ([468]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Lawfare-473]  Bulgaria supplied Ukraine with over one third of the ammunition needed in the early phase of the invasion and a plurality of needed fuel. [LINK: ([469]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-474]  Most of Ukraine's supporters ruled out sending troops to the country in the early months of the invasion. [LINK: ([470]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-475]

Although  [LINK: (India has maintained a neutral stance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India_and_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  on the invasion, [LINK: ([471]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-476]  reports indicate that artillery shells made by Indian manufacturers were sold to European countries and then diverted to Ukraine. Indian officials have not intervened despite objections from Russia. [LINK: ([472]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-reuters-2024-09-19-477]

The Russian government has threatened retaliation against countries supplying military aid to Ukraine, and said it meant NATO was waging a proxy war against Russia. [LINK: ([473]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-478]  According to the  [LINK: (Atlantic Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Council] 's Peter Dickinson, Russia's government has not followed through on its threats, despite most of its " [LINK: (red lines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_lines_in_the_Russo-Ukrainian_war] " being crossed. [LINK: ([474]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-479]  President Putin said that if military aid stopped, Ukraine would not survive for long. [LINK: ([475]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-480]

In May 2025, Ralph Goff, a former chief of operations at the  [LINK: (CIA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CIA] , said that the Biden administration gave Ukraine just enough weapons to bleed, but not to win, adding that "[They] allowed themselves to be bamboozled by Vladimir Putin and his nuclear-sabre rattling." [LINK: ([476]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-481]  [LINK: ([477]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-482]

Since the start of Russia's invasion in 2022, the United States has been sharing intelligence gathered through sophisticated satellites and manned and unmanned aerial systems with Ukraine, with a focus on finished signal intelligence to aid in targeting. [LINK: ([478]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-483]  [LINK: ([479]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-484]  In March 2025, the United States paused intelligence sharing with Ukraine, [LINK: ([480]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-485]  only to resume it a few days later. [LINK: ([481]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-486]  [LINK: ([482]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-487]  In October 2025, the Wall Street Journal and Reuters reported that the US was to provide intelligence to enable long-range strikes on Russian energy infrastructure deep in Russian territory. [LINK: ([483]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-488]  [LINK: ([484]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-489]  [LINK: ([485]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-490]  In November 2025, Reuters reported that the United States threatened to cut intelligence sharing in order to pressure Ukraine into negotiating a peace agreement. [LINK: ([486]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-491]

By January 2026, according to French president Macron, the  [LINK: (coalition of the willing) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coalition_of_the_willing_(Russo-Ukrainian_war)] , a coalition of 34 countries, were providing all of the international military support to Ukraine, with France providing two-thirds of the military intelligence. [LINK: ([487]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-492]  [LINK: ([488]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-493]

Belarus has allowed Russia to use its territory to stage part of the invasion, and to launch missiles into Ukraine. [LINK: ([489]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-494]  Belarus airspace was used by Russia, including for  [LINK: (radar early warning and control) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airborne_early_warning_and_control]  missions, until 2023, when a Russian  [LINK: (Beriev A-50) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beriev_A-50]  surveillance plane was  [LINK: (damaged by drones) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machulishchy_air_base_attack] . [LINK: ([490]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-495]  Belarus is considered a  [LINK: (co-belligerent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Co-belligerence] . [LINK: ([491]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-496]  [LINK: ([492]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-497]  [LINK: ([493]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-498]  Political scientist Matthew Frear considers Belarus a co- [LINK: (combatant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combatant] , with " [LINK: (Lukashenko) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lukashenko]  repeatedly stated his support for Putin's military actions", [LINK: ([494]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-499]  while the 2023 issue Armed Conflict Survey classified it as not a direct  [LINK: (co-combatant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combatant] . [LINK: ([495]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-500]  Belarus provided Russia with weapons and ammunition, and later, according to the 2024 issue of Armed Conflict Survey, Russia deployed tactical nuclear weapons in Belarus. [LINK: ([496]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-501]

In June 2023, US military intelligence suggested Iran was providing both  [LINK: (Shahed combat drones) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahed_drone]  and production materials to develop a  [LINK: (drone manufactory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yelabuga_drone_factory]  to Russia. [LINK: ([497]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-502]  In February 2024, a  [LINK: (Reuters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reuters]  report indicated that Iran sent ballistic missiles to the Russian military. [LINK: ([498]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-503]  According to the US and Ukraine, Iranian troops have been stationed in Crimea to assist Russia in launching drone attacks against civilians and civilian infrastructure. [LINK: ([499]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-504]  [LINK: ([500]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-505]  Iran has denied sending arms to Russia for use against Ukraine. [LINK: ([397]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-CNN:_Iran_denies_supplying_Russia_with_weapons_for_use_in_Ukraine-402]

[LINK: (North Korea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korea]  has supplied Russia with ballistic missiles and launchers. [LINK: ([501]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-506]  [LINK: ([502]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-507]  In October 2024, Ukraine and  [LINK: (South Korea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korea]  claimed North Korean engineers had been deployed to the battlefield to help with the launch of these missiles, and suffered casualties. [LINK: ([503]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-The_Guardian:_North_Korean_Troops_Deployed-508]  [LINK: ([504]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k284-509]  [LINK: ([505]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-a180-510]

Later the same month, a White House spokesperson said the US was "concerned" about reports North Korean soldiers were fighting for Russia. [LINK: ([506]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-u147-511]  [LINK: ([507]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-n959-512]  [LINK: ([508]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-h428-513]  Zelensky announced that Ukrainian intelligence believed there were 10,000 North Korean troops preparing to join Russian forces. [LINK: ([509]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-514]  The North Korean government stated that none of their soldiers were fighting for Russia. [LINK: ([510]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-515]  [LINK: ([511]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-516]  [LINK: ([512]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-517]

The US later said it had seen evidence that North Korea had sent 3,000 soldiers to Russia for possible deployment to Ukraine, determining that the soldiers had been transported by ship in October and were training at three military bases in eastern Russia. The US added that the alleged North Korean deployment could be further evidence that the Russian military was having problems with manpower. [LINK: ([513]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-y335-518]  On 28 October, NATO chief  [LINK: (Mark Rutte) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Rutte]  confirmed earlier Ukrainian intelligence that North Korean troops had been deployed to  [LINK: (Kursk Oblast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kursk_Oblast]  to support Russia against the  [LINK: (Kursk offensive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kursk_offensive_(2024%E2%80%932025)] , and the Pentagon reported that around 10,000 North Korean soldiers been sent in total. [LINK: ([514]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-519]  [LINK: ([515]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-i587-520]  [LINK: ([516]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k484-521]

On 7 November, Ukraine's defence minister reported that North Korean troops had engaged in battle on 5 November. [LINK: ([517]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-522]  On 13 November, the US State Department and South Korea confirmed that North Korean troops had engaged in combat against Ukrainian forces in Kursk Oblast. [LINK: ([518]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-r219-523]  [LINK: ([519]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-b215-524]  [LINK: ([520]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-u951-525]  [LINK: ([521]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-526]  On 24 November, the Ukrainian chief of general staff confirmed that North Korean troops had been engaged in battle. [LINK: ([522]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-527]  On 2 December the Pentagon stated that it had no evidence of North Korean troops engaged in combat, but noted that North Korean soldiers had been integrated into Russian units. [LINK: ([523]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-528]  The Ukrainian intelligence directorate confirmed that North Korean troops have been integrated into Russian units held in reserve, but said North Korean troops were unlikely to be engaged in combat and were still engaged in training. [LINK: ([524]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-529]  On 16 December, the US confirmed that North Korean troops had been killed in combat in Russia's Kursk oblast. [LINK: ([525]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-530]  By 18 December, the number of killed and wounded North Korean soldiers had reportedly reached a couple of hundred, [LINK: ([526]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-531]  while South Korea reported 100 North Koreans dead and 1000 injured. [LINK: ([527]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-532]   [LINK: (US Army JAG) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Army_Judge_Advocate_General%27s_Corps]  officers Steve Szymanski and Joshua Keruski stated that North Korea had become a party to an international armed conflict with Ukraine as of the 5 November engagement. [LINK: ([528]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Szymanski_&_Keruski-533]

Zelenskyy showcased footage which he said showed Russian troops burning the faces of killed North Korean soldiers to conceal their presence on the battlefield. [LINK: ([529]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-i614-534]  [LINK: ([530]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-q291-535]  [LINK: ([531]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-f970-536]  [LINK: ([532]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-d245-537]

In January 2025, two North Korean servicemen were taken prisoner while fighting in Kursk Oblast. Intelligence debriefings indicate that North Korean soldiers have been issued false military papers stating that they are Russians from  [LINK: (Tuva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuva] . [LINK: ([533]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nk-soldiers-captured-538]  In March 2025, South Korea's Joint Chiefs of Staff said that North Korea had sent an additional 3,000 soldiers early in the year, along with military equipment including short-range ballistic missiles. According to South Korea, roughly 11,000 North Korean soldiers had been sent to Russia, 4,000 of which were killed or wounded. [LINK: ([534]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-539]  The same month, Russia acknowledged the presence of North Korean soldiers helping its forces in Kursk for the first time, with  [LINK: (Valery Gerasimov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valery_Gerasimov]  expressing gratitude to North Korean troops for assisting in "liberating border areas of the Kursk Region". [LINK: ([535]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-x755-540]

In June 2025, former Russian defence minister Sergei Shoigu announced that 1,000 North Korean military workers would be deployed to Kursk for demining, and another 5,000 for reconstruction. [LINK: ([536]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-541]  The BBC reported that, in 2024, more than 10,000 North Korean workers were sent to work in "slave-like conditions" in Russian construction and other sectors, "in violation of the UN sanctions banning the use of North Korean labour." A South Korean intelligence official told the BBC that as many as 50,000 workers were to be deployed in 2025. [LINK: ([537]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-542]

[LINK: (Politico) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Politico]  reported in March 2023 that Chinese state-owned weapons manufacturer  [LINK: (Norinco) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norinco]  shipped assault rifles, drone parts, and body armour to Russia between June and December 2022, with some shipments via third countries including  [LINK: (Turkey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkey]  and the  [LINK: (United Arab Emirates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates] . [LINK: ([539]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-544]  According to the US, Chinese ammunition has been used on battlefields in Ukraine. [LINK: ([540]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-545]  In May 2023, the European Union identified that Chinese and UAE firms were supplying weapon components to Russia. [LINK: ([541]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-546]  In April 2024, China was reported to have provided Russia with  [LINK: (geospatial intelligence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geospatial_intelligence] , machine tools for tanks, and propellants for missiles. [LINK: ([542]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-547]  In September 2024, Reuters reported documents indicating Russia had established a weapons programme in China to develop and produce long-range attack drones, with assistance from local specialists, for use in the invasion. [LINK: ([543]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k324-548]  In July 2025, Ukraine's  [LINK: (Main Directorate of Intelligence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Directorate_of_Intelligence_(Ukraine)]  reported that  [LINK: (Laos) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laos]  had sent a 50-person demining crew to Kursk, though Laos denied the claim. [LINK: ([544]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-549]

Russia imports sensitive electronics, machinery, auto parts, and defence equipment from India. [LINK: ([545]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-550]  Trade like oil sales has surged since 2022, boosting revenue for Russian state-owned companies. To bypass sanctions and manage its currency surplus, Russia pays in rupees, supporting both civilian and military needs. [LINK: ([546]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-551]  [LINK: ([547]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-552]  Reuters reported in July 2025 that according to Indian customs data, an Indian company shipped military-use explosive compounds valued at $1.4 million to Russia in December 2024. [LINK: ([548]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-o455-553]

Russia has continued to make billions from fossil fuel exports to the West. Ukraine's Western allies[ [LINK: (which?) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Avoid_weasel_words] ] have paid Russia more for its hydrocarbons than they have given Ukraine in aid. In 2025  [LINK: (Slovakia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slovakia]  and  [LINK: (Hungary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hungary]  rejected an EU plan to phase out deliveries of Russian gas via Turkey by 2028. "Laundromat refineries" in Turkey and India process Russian crude and sell the refined fuel to sanctioning countries. [LINK: ([549]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-554]  [LINK: ([550]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-555]  [LINK: ([551]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-556]  Russia has also developed partnerships with  [LINK: (India) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India]  and  [LINK: (UAE) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UAE]  that actively support its efforts to evade sanctions. [LINK: ([552]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-:23-557]  [LINK: ([553]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-558]  [LINK: ([554]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-559]

In 2022, approximately 400,000 US-made semiconductors worth $53.6 million were shipped to Russia via  [LINK: (Maldives) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maldives] , accounting for almost 20% of Maldives's exports. Maldives has no native semiconductor manufacturers; all of its exports are by Russian shell companies, most headquartered in Hong Kong. [LINK: ([555]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-560]  [LINK: ([556]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-561]  From 2015 to 2021, average annual trade between Russia and Turkey in 45 military-linked materials was $28 million; from January to October 2023 it was $158 million. [LINK: ([557]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-ftTurkeyRussiaTrade-562]

From January 2022 to mid-2025, the United States imported $24.51 billion of Russian goods, mainly fertilisers,  [LINK: (enriched uranium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enriched_uranium]  and  [LINK: (plutonium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plutonium] , and  [LINK: (palladium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palladium] . [LINK: ([558]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-563]


# CASUALTIES #

Russian and Ukrainian sources have been said to inflate opposing casualty numbers and downplay their own losses for morale. [LINK: ([559]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-564]  Leaked US documents say "under-reporting of casualties within the [Russian] system highlights the military's 'continuing reluctance' to convey bad news up the chain of command." [LINK: ([560]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-565]  Russian news outlets have largely stopped reporting the Russian death toll. [LINK: ([561]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-fortune-566]  Russia and Ukraine have admitted suffering "significant" [LINK: ([562]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-tragedy-567]  and "considerable" losses, respectively. [LINK: ([563]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-untilwarends-568]  [LINK: ([564]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-569]

The numbers of civilian and military deaths have been  [LINK: (impossible to determine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog_of_war]  precisely. [LINK: ([565]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-570]   [LINK: (Agence France-Presse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agence_France-Presse]  (AFP) reported that neither it nor independent conflict monitors could verify Russian and Ukrainian claims of enemy losses and suspected they were inflated. [LINK: ([566]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-571]  In October 2022, the independent Russian media project  [LINK: (iStories) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Important_Stories] , citing sources close to the Kremlin, reported that more than 90,000 Russian soldiers had been killed, seriously wounded, or gone missing. [LINK: ([567]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-572]

While combat deaths can be inferred from a variety of sources, including  [LINK: (satellite imagery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_imagery]  of military action, measuring civilian deaths is more difficult. In June 2022, the  [LINK: (Ukrainian Minister of Defence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukrainian_Ministry_of_Defence]  told CNN that tens of thousands of Ukrainians had died, and he hoped the death toll was below 100,000. [LINK: ([568]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-573]  By July 2024, about 20,000 Ukrainians had lost limbs. [LINK: ([569]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-574]  In Mariupol, Ukrainian officials believe at least 25,000 have been killed, [LINK: ([570]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-575]  [LINK: ([571]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-576]  and bodies were still being discovered in September 2022. [LINK: ([572]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-577]  The mayor said over 10,000 and possibly as many as 20,000 civilians died in the  [LINK: (siege of Mariupol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Mariupol]  and Russian forces had brought mobile cremation equipment when they entered the city. [LINK: ([573]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-578]  [LINK: ([574]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-579]  [LINK: ([575]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-580]  An investigation by  [LINK: (AP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press]  from the end of 2022 estimated up to 75,000 killed civilians in the Mariupol area alone. [LINK: ([576]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-581]  [LINK: ([577]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-582]   [LINK: (AFP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agence_France-Presse]  says "a key gap in casualty counts is the lack of information from Russian-occupied places like the port city of Mariupol, where tens of thousands of civilians are believed to have died". [LINK: ([578]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-583]  There were at least 8,000 excess deaths in Mariupol between March 2022 and February 2023. [LINK: ([579]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-584]  The  [LINK: (Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_the_United_Nations_High_Commissioner_for_Human_Rights]  (OHCHR) believed the true civilian casualty numbers were significantly higher than it has been able to confirm. [LINK: ([580]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-OHCHR_sep-585]

In the Russian military, ethnic minorities have suffered disproportionately high casualties. In October 2022, the Russian regions with the highest death tolls were  [LINK: (Dagestan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dagestan] ,  [LINK: (Tuva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuva]  and  [LINK: (Buryatia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buryatia] , all minority regions. In February 2024, six out of ten Russian regions with the highest mortality rates in Ukraine were located in Siberia and the far east, and ethnic minorities' continuing outsized casualty rates prompted analysts to warn that the situation will lead to long-term destructive impacts on these communities. [LINK: ([581]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-n606-586]  [LINK: ([582]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-g456-587]  [LINK: ([583]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-g132-588]  [LINK: ([584]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-c079-589]  [LINK: ([585]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-j309-590]  About 1,200 Russian soldiers were killed or wounded in Ukraine every day in May–June 2024, [LINK: ([586]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-591]  which climbed to 1,500 by November 2024, when 45,690 casualties were estimated. [LINK: ([587]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-592]   [LINK: (Newsweek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newsweek]  estimated that on the bloodiest day in November 2024 the ratio of killed Russian men (1,950) exceeded the average daily male birth rate in Russia (1,836). [LINK: ([588]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-593]  Latvia-based news outlet  [LINK: (Meduza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meduza]  estimated that up to 140,000 Russian soldiers had died by July 2024. [LINK: ([589]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-594]  In December 2025, an analysis by the BBC suggested that Russian casualties were growing at a faster rate than at any point previously in the invasion, with 40% more obituaries of soldiers being published in Russia in 2025 compared to 2024. [LINK: ([590]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-r579-595]  [LINK: ([591]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-e211-596]

The Russian invasion became the deadliest European war in the last 80 years. [LINK: ([592]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-597]  Ukrainian average  [LINK: (mortality rate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_mortality_rate]  was 8.7/1000 people in 2020, [LINK: ([593]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-598]  and jumped to 18.6 in 2024, whereas Russia's mortality rate was 14/1000, ranking them as #1 and #9, of countries with the highest mortality rates. [LINK: ([594]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-599]  In August 2024,  [LINK: (Haaretz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haaretz]  estimated 172,000 people had died in the Russian invasion. [LINK: ([595]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-600]  In February 2025,  [LINK: (Quincy Institute for Responsible Statecraft) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quincy_Institute_for_Responsible_Statecraft]  estimated 250,000 dead. [LINK: ([596]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-601]  In September 2024, the  [LINK: (Wall Street Journal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall_Street_Journal]  reported that there were now one million Ukrainians and Russians who were killed or wounded. [LINK: ([597]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-WSJ-Casualties-602]


# WAR CRIMES AND ATTACKS ON CIVILIANS #

The Russian military and authorities have been responsible for deliberate  [LINK: (attacks against civilian targets) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attacks_on_civilians_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  [LINK: ([626]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-637]  (including  [LINK: (strikes on hospitals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_strikes_on_hospitals_during_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  and  [LINK: (on the energy grid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_strikes_against_Ukrainian_infrastructure_(2022%E2%80%93present)] ), massacres of civilians, abduction and torture of civilians,  [LINK: (sexual violence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexual_violence_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , [LINK: ([627]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-638]  forced deportation of civilians, and torture and murder of Ukrainian prisoners of war. They have also carried out  [LINK: (indiscriminate attacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiscriminate_attack]  in densely populated areas, including  [LINK: (with cluster bombs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Use_of_cluster_munitions_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , in one instance killing 61 people in the  [LINK: (Kramatorsk railway station attack) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kramatorsk_railway_station_attack] . [LINK: ([628]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-639]  [LINK: ([629]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-640]  [LINK: ([630]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-641]  [LINK: ([207]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-z362-212]  According to  [LINK: (Kyrylo Budanov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyrylo_Budanov] , the chief of the Ukrainian intelligence, Russia before the start of the invasion had created 'execution lists' of Ukrainian teachers, journalists, scientists, writers, priests, and politicians, and was preparing for a  [LINK: (genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide]  of Ukrainians; the plans included locations of  [LINK: (mass graves) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_grave]  and mobile  [LINK: (crematoria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crematorium] . [LINK: ([631]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-642]

According to the  [LINK: (United Nations' Human Rights Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_the_United_Nations_High_Commissioner_for_Human_Rights]  (OHCHR), by December 2023, about 78% of confirmed civilian casualties had been killed in Ukrainian-held territory. [LINK: ([633]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-644]  Over 12,300 civilians have been killed since the invasion began. [LINK: ([634]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-645]  Russia has deliberately and repeatedly attacked Ukrainian civilians with  [LINK: (FPV drones) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First-person_view_(radio_control)] , including  [LINK: (first responders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_responder] , [LINK: ([635]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-p704-646]  such as in the Kherson terror campaign dubbed the " [LINK: (human safari) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_safari_(terror_campaign)] ". [LINK: ([636]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-p249-647]  [LINK: ([637]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-z211-648]  [LINK: ([638]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-v798-649]  In October 2025, the UN concluded that the Russian military were systematically attacking Ukrainian civilians and civilian targets with drones along a 300-kilometre stretch of the  [LINK: (Dnipro River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dnipro_River] , to drive Ukrainians out of the region. The report said that these are war crimes and crimes against humanity. [LINK: ([639]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-OHCHR_report_Oct_25-650]

The UN Human Rights Office reports that Russia is committing severe  [LINK: (human rights violations in occupied Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian-occupied_territories_of_Ukraine#Violations_and_war_crimes] , including  [LINK: (arbitrary detentions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitrary_arrest_and_detention] ,  [LINK: (enforced disappearances) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enforced_disappearance] , torture, crackdown on  [LINK: (protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_protests_in_Russian-occupied_Ukraine]  and  [LINK: (freedom of speech) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freedom_of_speech] , enforced  [LINK: (Russification) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russification] , indoctrination of children, and suppression of Ukrainian language and culture. [LINK: ([640]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-UN_report_March_24-651]  The UN also found that Russian authorities were systematically deporting Ukrainian civilians from occupied provinces, which is a crime against humanity. [LINK: ([639]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-OHCHR_report_Oct_25-650]  Ukrainians have been  [LINK: (coerced into taking Russian passports) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passportization]  and becoming Russian citizens. Those who refuse are denied healthcare and other rights, [LINK: ([640]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-UN_report_March_24-651]  and can be imprisoned as a "foreign citizen". Ukrainian men who take Russian citizenship are drafted to fight against Ukraine. [LINK: ([641]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-652]

Russian forces have reportedly used banned  [LINK: (chemical weapons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical_weapons] , usually  [LINK: (tear gas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tear_gas]  grenades. [LINK: ([642]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Hambling_2023_z855-653]  [LINK: ([643]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Field_2024_s609-654]  In April 2024, a  [LINK: (Daily Telegraph) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Daily_Telegraph]  investigation concluded that "Russian troops are carrying out a systematic campaign of illegal chemical attacks against Ukrainian soldiers". [LINK: ([644]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-The_Telegraph_2024_g413-655]

In January 2026, the UN reported that 2025 had been the deadliest year for Ukrainian civilians since 2022, with 2,514 deaths and 12,142 injuries being verified during the year, a 31% increase compared to 2024. The report said that a ″massive increase″ of Russian long-range weapons was causing increased harm in urban centres. [LINK: ([645]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-d405-656]  [LINK: ([646]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-l054-657]

An August 2022 report by the Humanitarian Research Lab of the  [LINK: (Yale School of Public Health) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_School_of_Public_Health]  identified 21  [LINK: (filtration camps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_filtration_camps_for_Ukrainians]  for Ukrainian "civilians, POWs [prisoners of war], and other personnel" in the vicinity of Donetsk oblast. Imaging of one camp,  [LINK: (Olenivka prison) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olenivka,_Kalmiuske_Raion] , found two sites of disturbed earth consistent with "potential graves". [LINK: ([647]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Yale_School_of_Medicine-658]  Kaveh Khoshnood, a professor at the Yale School of Public Health, said: "Incommunicado detention of civilians is more than a violation of international humanitarian law—it represents a threat to the public health of those currently in the custody of Russia and its proxies." Conditions described by freed prisoners include exposure, insufficient access to sanitation, food and water, cramped conditions, electrical shocks and assault. [LINK: ([647]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Yale_School_of_Medicine-658]

An  [LINK: (OHCHR) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OHCHR]  report released in November 2022 documented abuses on both sides, based on interviews with prisoners. [LINK: ([648]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-659]  In March 2023, UN human rights commissioner  [LINK: (Volker Türk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volker_T%C3%BCrk]  reported that more than 90% of the Ukrainian POWs interviewed by his office said they had been tortured or ill-treated, including "welcoming beatings" on their arrival in penitentiary facilities, which Russia despite several requests did not give UN staff access to. [LINK: ([649]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-660]

In April 2023, several videos circulated on different websites purportedly showing Russian soldiers beheading Ukrainian soldiers. [LINK: ([650]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-AP_NEWS_2023-661]

In March 2024, the UN issued a report saying Russia may have executed more than 30 recently captured Ukrainian POWs over the winter. The UN Human Rights Office verified three incidents in which Russian servicemen executed seven Ukrainian servicemen. According to the same report, 39 of 60 released Ukrainian POWs "disclosed that they had been subjected to sexual violence during their internment, including attempted rape, threats of rape and castration, beatings or the administration of electric shocks to genitals, and repeated forced nudity, including during interrogations". [LINK: ([651]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Melkozerova_2024_g984-662]

In October 2024, the  [LINK: (EEAS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_External_Action_Service]  released a statement describing the increasing frequency of Russian executions of Ukrainian prisoners, with at least 177 prisoners dying in Russian captivity since the beginning of the war. The statement included the OHCHR's confirmation of systematic use of different methods of torture used by Russians against Ukrainian prisoners. [LINK: ([652]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-m314-663]

The Ukrainian Armed Forces have been accused of executions and other abuses of Russian POWs, but the number of allegations has been significantly lower. [LINK: ([653]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Report_OHCHR-664] : para. 105  [LINK: ([654]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-NYT-Russian-POW-665]  [LINK: ([655]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-666]  [LINK: ([656]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-667]

In June 2024, an investigation by the  [LINK: (Financial Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_Times]  identified four Ukrainian children on a Russian government-linked adoption website who had been abducted from state care homes. The children's Ukrainian background was not mentioned. One child was shown with a new Russian name and an age that differed from their Ukrainian documents, while another was shown using a Russian version of their Ukrainian name. 17 other matches identified by the Financial Times on the adoption website were confirmed as Ukrainian children in a New York Times investigation. Ukrainian authorities estimate that nearly 20,000 Ukrainian children have been forcibly taken from occupied territories to Russia since the full-scale invasion began. Wayne Jordash, president of humanitarian law firm Global Rights Compliance, described forcibly transferring or deporting children as war crimes, adding that when done as part of a widespread or systematic attack on a civilian population, Russia is also committing crimes against humanity. [LINK: ([657]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-k182-668]  [LINK: ([658]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-t017-669]

A March 2025 report published by the ISW suggests that the mass transfer of Ukrainian children to Russia had been an established component of Russia's war plans in Ukraine prior to the invasion. It references Russian governmental documents that predate the invasion by days which outlined plans to move Ukrainian orphans in occupied Ukraine to Russia described internally as 'humanitarian evacuations'. Since the invasion, tens to hundreds of thousands of Ukrainian children have been deported to Russia, where they have been housed in camps, placed with adoptive families, and provided with new Russian identities including new or altered names, falsified birth certificates, and other state documentation. The report claims that Russia intends to convert the abductees into 'the next generation of Russians' whilst 'depriving [Ukraine] of its multi-generation potential'. Referencing Article 2 of the  [LINK: (Genocide Convention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide_Convention] , the author concludes the opening section by noting that 'International law explicitly forbids the forcible transfer of children from one group to another group for the purpose of destroying, in whole or in part, a national or ethnic group, and considers these violations as constituent acts of genocide.' [LINK: ([659]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-o490-670]

In August 2025,  [LINK: (Mykola Kuleba) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mykola_Kuleba] , the head of a Ukrainian  [LINK: (NGO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-governmental_organization] , said that Russian occupation authorities in Ukraine had created an online "catalog" of Ukrainian children up for adoption sorted by physical traits such as hair and eye colour, denouncing the practice as  [LINK: (child trafficking) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trafficking_of_children] . [LINK: ([660]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-r419-671]  [LINK: ([661]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-n637-672]  [LINK: ([662]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-s452-673]  [LINK: ([663]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-b387-674]

The  [LINK: (International Criminal Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court]  (ICC) opened  [LINK: (an investigation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court_investigation_in_Ukraine]  into possible  [LINK: (crimes against humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity] ,  [LINK: (genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegations_of_genocide_of_Ukrainians_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  and war crimes committed in Ukraine. [LINK: ([664]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-675]  On 17 March 2023, the ICC issued a warrant for Putin's arrest, charging him with individual criminal responsibility in the  [LINK: (abduction of children) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Child_abductions_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  forcibly deported to Russia. [LINK: ([665]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-issues_warrant_2023_03_17_reuters-676]  It was the first time that the ICC had issued an arrest warrant for the head of state of a  [LINK: (permanent member) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permanent_members_of_the_United_Nations_Security_Council]  of the  [LINK: (United Nations Security Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council]  [LINK: ([665]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-issues_warrant_2023_03_17_reuters-676]  (the world's five principal nuclear powers). [LINK: ([666]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nuclear_powers-677]  Moscow has denied any involvement in war crimes, a response Vittorio Bufacchi of  [LINK: (University College Cork) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_College_Cork]  says "has bordered on the farcical", [LINK: ([667]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-678]  and its contention that the images coming out of Bucha were fabricated "a disingenuous response born by delusional hubris, post-truth on overdrive, (that) does not merit to be taken seriously." Even the usually fractured  [LINK: (United States Senate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Senate]  came together to call Putin a war criminal. [LINK: ([668]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-679]

One of several efforts to document Russian war crimes concerns its repeated bombardment of markets and bread lines, destruction of basic infrastructure and attacks on exports and supply convoys, in a country where deliberate starvation of Ukrainians by Soviets (the  [LINK: (Holodomor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holodomor] ) still looms large in public memory. [LINK: ([669]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-680]  Forcible deportation of populations, such as took place in Mariupol, is another area of focus: [LINK: ([670]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-681]

forced deportations and transfers are defined both as war crimes under the  [LINK: (Fourth Geneva Convention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourth_Geneva_Convention]  and  [LINK: (Protocol II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protocol_II]  and Article 8 of the Rome Statute—and as crimes against humanity—under Article 7 of the  [LINK: (Rome Statute) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rome_Statute] . As both war crimes and  [LINK: (crimes against humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crime_against_humanity] , they have several mechanisms for individual accountability, the International Criminal Court and also, at the individual state level,  [LINK: (universal jurisdiction) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_jurisdiction]  and  [LINK: (Magnitsky sanctions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnitsky_legislation]  legislation.

The ICC issued arrest warrants for military officials  [LINK: (Sergey Kobylash) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergey_Kobylash] ,  [LINK: (Viktor Sokolov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Sokolov_(naval_officer)] ,  [LINK: (Sergei Shoigu) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergei_Shoigu]  and  [LINK: (Valery Gerasimov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valery_Gerasimov] . [LINK: ([671]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-682]  [LINK: ([672]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-WarCrime-683]


# IMPACTS #

The invasion contributed to the  [LINK: (2022 food crises) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022%E2%80%932023_global_food_crises] . [LINK: ([673]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-684]  As of February 2025, 3.7 million Ukrainians were internally displaced and 6.9 million were refugees. [LINK: ([674]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-685]

Russian attacks on civilians, causing mass civilian casualties and displacement, have been characterised as  [LINK: (genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide]  and  [LINK: (democide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democide] . [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Etkind-31]  [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-h710-32]  [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-q611-33]  [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-v008-34]  In September 2023, a UN-mandated investigative body reported that Russian occupiers had tortured Ukrainians to death, and forced families to listen as they raped women. [LINK: ([675]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Farge_2023_r049-686]  The commission previously found that violations committed by Russian forces in Ukraine may constitute  [LINK: (crimes against humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity] . [LINK: ([676]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Gabrielle_Tétrault-Farber_2023_t397-687]

By August 2024, the  [LINK: (WHO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WHO]  had recorded 1,940  [LINK: (attacks against Ukrainian healthcare) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_strikes_on_hospitals_during_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  and reported widespread  [LINK: (double-tap) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_tap_strike]  attacks. [LINK: ([677]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-688]  In 2023,  [LINK: (Physicians for Human Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physicians_for_Human_Rights]  described Russian attacks on Ukraine's healthcare system as having a "reasonable basis" to be considered war crimes, and could potentially constitute crimes against humanity. [LINK: ([678]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-PHR_2023_i908-689]

As of August 2025,  [LINK: (UNESCO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO]  has verified damage to 508 Ukrainian cultural sites, including 151 religious sites, 34 museums, 33 monuments, and 18 libraries. [LINK: ([679]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-690]  In 2022, the  [LINK: (European Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Parliament]  labelled Russia's destruction of Ukrainian cultural property a war crime. [LINK: ([680]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-691]  Ukraine's  [LINK: (Minister of Culture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Culture_and_Strategic_Communications]   [LINK: (Oleksandr Tkachenko) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oleksandr_Tkachenko_(journalist)]  called it  [LINK: (cultural genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_genocide] . [LINK: ([681]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-692]

The war has caused the largest refugee and  [LINK: (humanitarian crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humanitarian_crisis]  in Europe since World War II. [LINK: ([682]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-693]  In the first week of the invasion, the UN reported over a million refugees had fled Ukraine; this reached over eight million by February 2023. [LINK: ([683]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-UNHCR-Ukraine-694]  [LINK: ([684]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-695]  In May 2022, following an influx of military equipment into Ukraine, a significant number of refugees sought to return to regions relatively isolated from the front in southeastern Ukraine. [LINK: ([685]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-696]  By 3 May, another 8 million people were displaced inside Ukraine. [LINK: ([686]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-697]

Most refugees were women, children, elderly, or disabled. [LINK: ([687]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-698]  Most male Ukrainian nationals aged 18 to 60 were denied exit from Ukraine as part of  [LINK: (mandatory conscription) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conscription_in_Ukraine] , [LINK: ([688]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-699]  unless they were responsible for the financial support of three or more children, single fathers, or were the parent/guardian of children with disabilities. [LINK: ([689]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-700]  Many Ukrainian men, including teenagers, opted to remain in Ukraine voluntarily to join the resistance. [LINK: ([690]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-701]

According to the UN High Commission for Refugees as of May 2022, there were 3,315,711 refugees in Poland, 901,696 in Romania, 594,664 in Hungary, 461,742 in Moldova, 415,402 in Slovakia, and 27,308 in Belarus, while Russia reported it had received over 800,104 refugees. [LINK: ([683]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-UNHCR-Ukraine-694]  By July 2022, over 390,000 Ukrainian refugees had arrived in the Czech Republic, where the average refugee was a woman accompanied by a child. [LINK: ([691]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-702]  Turkey registered more than 58,000 Ukrainian refugees as of March 2022. [LINK: ([692]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-703]  The EU invoked the  [LINK: (Temporary Protection Directive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporary_Protection_Directive]  for the first time, granting Ukrainian refugees the right to live and work in the EU for up to three years. [LINK: ([693]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-704]  Britain has accepted 146,379 refugees, as well as extending the ability to remain in the UK for three years. [LINK: ([694]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-705]

According to the  [LINK: (Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organization_for_Security_and_Co-operation_in_Europe]  (OSCE), Russia has engaged in "massive deportation" of over 1.3 million Ukrainian civilians, potentially constituting crimes against humanity. [LINK: ([695]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-706]  The OSCE and Ukraine have accused Russia of  [LINK: (forcibly moving) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forced_displacement]  civilians to filtration camps in Russian-held territory, and then into Russia. Ukrainian sources have compared this policy to  [LINK: (Soviet-era population transfers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Population_transfer_in_the_Soviet_Union]  and Russian actions in the  [LINK: (Chechen War of Independence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chechen_War_of_Independence] . [LINK: ([696]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Mackintosh-2022-04-08-707]  For instance, as of April 2022, Russia claimed to have evacuated about 121,000 Mariupol residents to Russia. [LINK: ([696]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Mackintosh-2022-04-08-707]  [LINK: ([697]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-708]   [LINK: (RIA Novosti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RIA_Novosti]  and Ukrainian officials said that thousands were dispatched to centres in cities in Russia and Russian-occupied Ukraine, [LINK: ([698]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-709]  from which people were sent to economically depressed regions of Russia. [LINK: ([699]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-710]  [LINK: ([700]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-711]  [LINK: ([l]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-713]

Both  [LINK: (Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aging_of_Russia]  and  [LINK: (Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demographics_of_Ukraine#Population_decline]  faced the prospect of significant  [LINK: (population decline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Population_decline]  even before the war. It is the first time that two countries with an average age above 40 have gone to war against each other. [LINK: ([702]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-714]  Russia had a fighting-age (18-to-40-year-old) male population more than four times higher than Ukraine's and slightly higher birth rates, while the willingness to fight was more pronounced in Ukraine. [LINK: ([703]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-715]

Several sources have pointed out that the war is considerably worsening  [LINK: (Ukraine's demographic crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukraine%27s_demographic_crisis] , making significant shrinking very likely. [LINK: ([704]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-716]  A July 2023 study by the  [LINK: (Vienna Institute for International Economic Studies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Vienna_Institute_for_International_Economic_Studies]  stated that, [LINK: ([705]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-kna23-717]

regardless of how long the war lasts and whether or not there is further military escalation, Ukraine is unlikely to recover demographically from the consequences of the war. Even in 2040 it will have only about 35 million inhabitants, around 20% fewer than before the war (2021: 42.8 million) and the decline in the working-age population is likely to be the most severe and far-reaching.

Flight from war affected especially the southern and eastern regions and especially educated women of child-bearing age and their children. With an estimate of more than 20% of refugees not returning, study author Maryna Tverdostup concluded that long-term shrinking will significantly impair reconstruction. [LINK: ([705]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-kna23-717]

The war in Ukraine and the associated emigration, lower  [LINK: (birth rates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birth_rate]  and war-related casualties further deepened the  [LINK: (demographic crisis of Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demographic_crisis_of_Russia] . [LINK: ([706]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-718]  The UN is projecting that the decline that started in 2021 will continue, and if current demographic conditions persist, Russia's population would be 120 million in fifty years, a decline of about 17%. [LINK: ([707]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-719]  [LINK: ([708]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-720]  Since February 2022, hundreds of thousands of  [LINK: (Russians have emigrated) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_emigration_following_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] ; estimates range from 370,000 to over 820,000. Combined with mobilisation, this possibly removed roughly half a million to one million working-age males from Russia's population. [LINK: ([709]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-721]  Studies report that this will have a demographic effect, especially in Russia, that lasts much longer than the conflict. [LINK: ([710]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-722]

According to BBC: [LINK: ([711]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-723]

They come from different walks of life. Some are journalists like us, but there are also IT experts, designers, artists, academics, lawyers, doctors, PR specialists, and linguists. Most are under 50. Many share western liberal values and hope Russia will be a democratic country one day. Some are LGBTQ+. Sociologists studying the current Russian emigration say there is evidence that those leaving are younger, better educated and wealthier than those staying. More often they are from bigger cities.

According to Johannes Wachs, "The exodus of skilled human capital, sometimes called  [LINK: (brain drain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brain_drain] , out of Russia may have a significant effect on the course of the war and the Russian economy in the long run." [LINK: ([712]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-724]  According to a survey, around 15 percent of those who left returned to Russia, either permanently or to settle their affairs. [LINK: ([713]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-725]  In November 2023, at the  [LINK: (World Russian People's Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Russian_People%27s_Council] , Putin urged Russian women to have eight or more children. [LINK: ([714]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Sharma_2023_w549-726]

In July 2024,  [LINK: (Chief of the General Staff) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_of_the_General_Staff_(United_Kingdom)]  of the British Army  [LINK: (Roland Walker) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roland_Walker]  said that with the current way of fighting, it would take Russia five years to control the four oblasts of Donetsk, Luhansk, Kherson and Zaporizhzhia that Russia claims as its own, and it would cost Russia from 1.5 to 1.8 million casualties. [LINK: ([715]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-727]

Based on a preliminary assessment, the war has inflicted $51 billion in environmental damage in Ukraine; according to a report by the  [LINK: (Yale School of the Environment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_School_of_the_Environment] , 623,000 tonnes (1.4 billion pounds) of petrochemicals have burned as a result of shelling, while nearly 1,500 tonnes (3.2 million pounds) of pollutants have leaked into bodies of water. Hazardous chemicals have contaminated around 28 hectares (70 acres) of soil, and likely made agricultural activities temporarily impossible. [LINK: ([716]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-enviroyale-728]  Around 30% of Ukraine's land is littered with explosives and more than 2.4 million hectares (5.9 million acres) of forest have been damaged. [LINK: ([717]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-729]

According to peace organisation PAX, Russia's "deliberate targeting of industrial and energy infrastructure" has caused "severe" pollution, and the use of explosive weapons has left "millions of tonnes" of contaminated debris in cities and towns. [LINK: ([718]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-pax-730]  In early June 2023, the  [LINK: (Kakhovka Dam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kakhovka_Dam] , under Russian occupation,  [LINK: (was damaged) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Destruction_of_the_Kakhovka_Dam] , causing flooding and triggering warnings of an "ecological disaster". [LINK: ([719]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-731]

The Ukrainian government, international observers and journalists have described the damage as  [LINK: (ecocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecocide] . [LINK: ([720]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-732]  The Ukrainian government is investigating more crimes against the environment and ecocide (a crime in Ukraine). [LINK: ([721]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-733]  Zelenskyy has met with prominent European figures ( [LINK: (Heidi Hautala) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heidi_Hautala] ,  [LINK: (Margot Wallstrom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margot_Wallstrom] ,  [LINK: (Mary Robinson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Robinson]  and  [LINK: (Greta Thunberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greta_Thunberg] ) to discuss the environmental damage and how to prosecute it. [LINK: ([722]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-734]

According to an investigation by NGL Media published in April 2024, Russia has completely destroyed over 60,000 hectares (150,000 acres) of Ukrainian forests. The investigation stated that long-term ecological consequences may include lowering of the groundwater level, reduction of biodiversity, worsening of air quality, fire outbreaks, and rivers and ponds drying up. [LINK: ([723]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Fornusek_2024_r049-735]

The war in Ukraine has severely disrupted global  [LINK: (climate policy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_policy]  and increased CO2 emissions. [LINK: ([724]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-736]  [LINK: ([725]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-737]  The effects have been strongly felt in Asia, [LINK: ([726]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-738]  [LINK: ([727]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-739]  [LINK: ([728]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-740]  Europe, [LINK: ([729]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-741] 
and the US. [LINK: ([730]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-742]   [LINK: (Fatih Birol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatih_Birol] , the head of the  [LINK: (International Energy Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Energy_Agency]  talking about the prospects of  [LINK: (COP 28) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_United_Nations_Climate_Change_Conference]  noted: [LINK: ([731]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-743]

... the geopolitical situation, with many nations at loggerheads over the  [LINK: (war in Ukraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_War] , and still frosty relations between the  [LINK: (US) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States]  and  [LINK: (China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China] , would make for a difficult summit. [...] The most important challenge [to limiting temperature rises to 1.5 °C (2.7 °F) above pre-industrial levels] is the lack of  [LINK: (international cooperation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_cooperation] .

Putin implied that Russia may use nuclear weapons if certain  [LINK: ("red lines") -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_lines_in_the_Russo-Ukrainian_war]  were crossed. By 2024, most of the  [LINK: (Russian government's "red lines") -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_lines_in_the_Russo-Ukrainian_war]  had been crossed without nuclear weapons being used in response. [LINK: ([732]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-744]

Four days into the invasion, Putin put Russia's nuclear forces on high alert, raising fears that Russia could use  [LINK: (tactical nuclear weapons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tactical_nuclear_weapon]  against Ukraine. [LINK: ([733]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-745]  In response to what he called "completely irresponsible actions", Zelenskyy suggested that there should be "global control" of Russia's nuclear assets. [LINK: ([734]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-746]  In March 2023, Putin announced plans to install Russian tactical nuclear weapons in Belarus. [LINK: ([735]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-747]

The invasion had an  [LINK: (impact on Ukraine's nuclear power plants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impact_of_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine_on_nuclear_power_plants] . Russian forces  [LINK: (captured Chernobyl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capture_of_Chernobyl]  on the first day, leading to a huge spike in radiation levels. [LINK: ([736]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-748]  Russia also captured  [LINK: (Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaporizhzhia_Nuclear_Power_Plant] , the largest in Europe, which has since  [LINK: (been at risk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaporizhzhia_Nuclear_Power_Plant_crisis]  and damaged by shelling. In August 2022, Zelenskyy described the situation as "Russian  [LINK: (nuclear terror) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_terrorism] ". [LINK: ([737]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-749]  The  [LINK: (International Atomic Energy Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Atomic_Energy_Agency]  said it was the first time a military conflict occurred amid nuclear plants, and it called for a demilitarised zone around Zaporizhzhia NPP. [LINK: ([738]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-750]

[LINK: (Ukrainian Minister of Economic Development and Trade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Economy_(Ukraine)]   [LINK: (Yulia Svyrydenko) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yulia_Svyrydenko]  announced that for 2022 Ukraine had a 30% loss in their  [LINK: (gross domestic product) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gross_domestic_product]  (GDP). [LINK: ([739]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-751]  The  [LINK: (International Monetary Fund) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Monetary_Fund]  predicted that Ukraine's GDP would decrease between 10% to 35%; [LINK: ([740]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-752]  the  [LINK: (European Bank for Reconstruction and Development) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Bank_for_Reconstruction_and_Development]  predicted a 20% decrease. [LINK: ([741]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-753]  The Ukrainian statistics service said that the GDP of Ukraine in 2023 grew by 5.3%. [LINK: ([742]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-754]

Ukraine began issuing  [LINK: (war bonds) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_bond]  on 1 March 2022, and the following day the Ukrainian government announced that they had raised 6.14 billion hryvnias. [LINK: ([743]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-755]  In May 2022 the European Commission banned grain sales in Bulgaria, Poland, Hungary, Romania and Slovakia, with the only exception being if they were transiting through those countries; the ban was lifted in September 2023. [LINK: ([744]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-756]

The war has caused a major  [LINK: (humanitarian crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humanitarian_crisis]  in Ukraine: the  [LINK: (United Nations Development Programme) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Development_Programme]  calculated in March 2022 that a prolonged conflict would cause 30% of the Ukrainian population to fall below the poverty line, while a further 62% would be at risk of also falling into poverty within a year. [LINK: ([745]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-757]

The US government estimates that Russia's economic losses from the war and Western sanctions will amount to around $1.3 trillion by 2025, and Russia's direct financial spending on the invasion is estimated at $250 billion (as of late 2024) – costs that Russia could not have foreseen. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DU-36]

The  [LINK: (Russian Ministry of Economic Development) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Economic_Development_(Russia)]  said that for 2022 the GDP contracted by 2.1% [LINK: ([746]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-758]  and for 2023 Russia's government said the GDP grew by 3.6%. [LINK: ([747]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-759]  On 27 April 2024, it was reported that Russia was planning tax increases to help finance the war. [LINK: ([748]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-760]  In January 2025, it was reported that, since early 2022, Russia had used a two-prong strategy to finance the war. In addition to the official Russian government  [LINK: (defence budget) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_budget_of_Russia] —direct financial expenditure for waging the war in Ukraine was estimated at US$250 billion through June 2024 for military spending through normal channels, [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DU-36]  with the military budget rising to over 20% of annual GDP—an additional off-budget financing mechanism was employed with over US$200 billion of debt funding obtained from preferential bank loans made to defence contractors and war-related businesses, loans that had been compelled by the Russian government. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-mt20250114-37]  [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-kpost20250115-38]

A report published in April 2025 by the Stockholm Institute of Transition Economics (SITE) noted that official Russian statistics are unreliable and opaque, and "serves to bolster the domestic narrative of economic resilience and questions the effectiveness of sanctions while masking underlying fragilities". [LINK: ([749]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-SITEreport1-761]  The report, which was presented to the finance ministers of the EU at a meeting of the  [LINK: (Economic and Financial Affairs Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_and_Financial_Affairs_Council]  on 13 May 2025, also argued that Russian fiscal stimulus has kept the economy afloat so far, but that it is on an unsustainable trajectory. [LINK: ([749]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-SITEreport1-761]  [LINK: ([750]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-sitepresent-762]  "Even under optimistic growth scenarios, Russia's economic scale remains insufficient to match its Western adversaries' capacity in a sustained geopolitical rivalry", the report concluded. [LINK: ([749]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-SITEreport1-761]  In August 2025,  [LINK: (VEB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VEB.RF] , one of the largest Russian state banks, assessed that the Russian economy had started slipping into recession. [LINK: ([751]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-e976-763]

A price cap was placed on Russian oil by the  [LINK: (Group of 7) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G7]  (G7) at $60 on 5 December 2022. [LINK: ([752]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-EI-764]  The US banned all imports of Russian oil on 8 March 2022. [LINK: ([753]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-765]  The European Union placed an embargo on oil products from Russia on 5 February 2023. [LINK: ([752]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-EI-764]  Other countries that embargoed Russian oil included  [LINK: (Five Eyes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Five_Eyes]  partners Canada, the United Kingdom, Australia and New Zealand. [LINK: ([754]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-766]  Russia issued a ban on foreign diesel sales starting on 21 September 2023, lifting it on 6 October. [LINK: ([755]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-767]

According to a study published in mid-2022 by Canadian think tank SecDev, Russia seized energy resources, metals and minerals worth at least $12.4 trillion in the Donbas. The total value of raw material stocks in Ukraine is estimated at over $26 trillion. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-DU-36]


# PEACE EFFORTS #

Peace negotiations between Russia and Ukraine took place on 28 February, 3 March, and 7 March 2022, on the  [LINK: (Belarus–Ukraine border) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belarus%E2%80%93Ukraine_border] , with further talks held on 10 March in Turkey and a fourth round of negotiations beginning 14 March. [LINK: ([756]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-768]  The talks ended without agreement.

In 2024, Ukraine's main peace terms were that Russia withdraw its troops, that Ukrainian prisoners be released, Russian leaders be prosecuted for  [LINK: (war crimes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_crimes_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] , and Ukraine be given security guarantees to prevent further aggression. Russia's main terms were that Russia must keep  [LINK: (all the land it occupied) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian-occupied_territories_of_Ukraine] , that it be given all of the  [LINK: (provinces that it claims) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_annexation_of_Donetsk,_Kherson,_Luhansk_and_Zaporizhzhia_oblasts]  but does not fully control, that Ukraine end plans to join NATO, and sanctions against Russia be lifted. [LINK: ([758]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Putin_terms-770]  According to Western sources, allowing Russia to keep the land it seized would " [LINK: (reward the aggressor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_of_conquest]  while punishing the victim" and set a dangerous precedent. [LINK: ([759]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Chatham_end-771]  They predicted this would allow Russia to re-arm and encourage it "to continue its  [LINK: (imperialist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_neo-imperialism]  campaign of expansionism" against Ukraine and other neighbours, and embolden other expansionist regimes. [LINK: ([759]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Chatham_end-771]  [LINK: ([760]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-772]  [LINK: ([761]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-773]  [LINK: ([762]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-774]

After  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  became US president in 2025, there was a major shift in US policy. The Trump administration began negotiations with Russia and separately with Ukraine. In February 2025, the US twice sided with Russia in UN resolutions, opposing a European-drafted resolution condemning Russia's actions and supporting the territorial integrity of Ukraine, and then drafting and voting for a UN Security Council resolution calling for the end of the conflict, but containing no criticism of Russia. [LINK: ([763]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-u697-775]

In November 2025, Trump adopted a 28-point peace plan for ending the war. The plan was interpreted as broadly pro-Russian, [LINK: ([764]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-e610-776]  [LINK: ([765]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-l915-777]  and according to  [LINK: (The Insider) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Insider_(website)] , was at its core a recycled Russian document substantially written by  [LINK: (Kirill Dmitriev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirill_Dmitriev] , a Kremlin operative. [LINK: ([766]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-j465-778]


# INTERNATIONAL REACTIONS #

The invasion received widespread international condemnation from  [LINK: (governments and intergovernmental organisations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_and_intergovernmental_reactions_to_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] . [LINK: ([767]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-779]  In March 2022 and February 2023, 141  [LINK: (member states) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Member_states_of_the_United_Nations]  of the  [LINK: (UN General Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UN_General_Assembly]  voted for a resolution that Russia should immediately withdraw. Seven, including Russia, opposed the measure. [LINK: ([768]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-780]  Political reactions to the invasion included  [LINK: (new sanctions on Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_during_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War] , which triggered  [LINK: (widespread economic effects on the Russian and world economies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_impact_of_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] . [LINK: ([769]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Sanctions_slams_Russian_economy-781]  As of July 2025, the EU had adopted 18 packages of sanctions against Russia and Belarus, totalling over 2,500 listed entities and persons. [LINK: ([770]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-EU18sanctions-782]  Sanctions forced Russia to reorient its oil exports, rely more on  [LINK: (LNG) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquefied_natural_gas]  (which was not subject to EU sanctions), and shift its coal exports from Europe to Asia. [LINK: ([771]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-783]  Most European countries cancelled nuclear cooperation with Russia. [LINK: ([772]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-784]

Over 70 countries and the European Union delivered  [LINK: (humanitarian aid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_humanitarian_aid_to_Ukraine_during_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War]  to Ukraine, and nearly 50 countries plus the EU provided  [LINK: (military aid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_military_aid_to_Ukraine_during_the_Russo-Ukrainian_War] . [LINK: ([773]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-785]  Economic sanctions included bans on Russian aircraft using EU airspace, [LINK: ([774]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-786]   [LINK: (certain Russian banks from the SWIFT payments system) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWIFT_ban_against_Russian_banks] , and certain Russian media outlets. [LINK: ([775]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-787]  Reactions to the invasion have included public and media responses,  [LINK: (peace efforts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Russia%E2%80%93Ukraine_peace_negotiations] , and the examination of the  [LINK: (invasion's legality) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legality_of_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine] .

Demonstrations were held worldwide, including  [LINK: (in Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_anti-war_protests_in_Russia]  and  [LINK: (parts of Ukraine occupied by Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_protests_in_Russian-occupied_Ukraine] . [LINK: ([776]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-788]  Calls for  [LINK: (a boycott of Russian goods) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_boycott_of_Russia_and_Belarus]  spread on social media, [LINK: ([777]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-789]  while hackers attacked Russian websites, particularly those operated by the Russian government. [LINK: ([778]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-790]   [LINK: (Anti-Russian sentiment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-Russian_sentiment]  against Russians living abroad surged after the invasion. [LINK: ([779]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-791]  In March 2022, Russian president Putin introduced  [LINK: (prison sentences of up to 15 years) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_2022_war_censorship_laws]  for publishing "fake news" about Russian military operations, [LINK: ([780]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-792]  intended to suppress any criticism related to the war. [LINK: ([781]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-793]

Some countries, particularly in the  [LINK: (Global South) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_North_and_Global_South] , saw public sympathy or outright support for Russia, due in part to distrust of  [LINK: (US foreign policy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_United_States] . [LINK: ([782]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-nyt-eligon-794]  According to the Economist Intelligence Unit in 2023, 31 percent of the world's population live in countries that are leaning towards or supportive of Russia, 30.7 percent live in neutral countries, and 36.2 percent live in countries that are against Russia in some way. [LINK: ([783]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-795]

A number of supranational and national parliaments passed resolutions declaring Russia to be a  [LINK: (state sponsor of terrorism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_sponsor_of_terrorism] . [LINK: ([784]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-796]  By October 2022, Latvia, Lithuania and Estonia had declared Russia a  [LINK: (terrorist state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_terrorism] . [LINK: ([785]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Fiedler_2022-797]  On 1 August 2023,  [LINK: (Iceland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iceland]  became the first European country to close its embassy in Russia as a result of the invasion. [LINK: ([786]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Nova_2023_y784-798]

The invasion prompted Ukraine, [LINK: ([787]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-799]   [LINK: (Finland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finland]  and  [LINK: (Sweden) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweden]  to apply for NATO membership. [LINK: ([788]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-800]  Finland became a member in April 2023, [LINK: ([789]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-801]  followed by Sweden in March 2024. [LINK: ([790]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-802]

A documentary film produced during the siege of Mariupol,  [LINK: (20 Days in Mariupol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/20_Days_in_Mariupol] , won the  [LINK: (Oscar for best documentary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Academy_Award_for_Best_Documentary_Feature_Film]  in  [LINK: (2024) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/97th_Academy_Awards] . [LINK: ([791]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russo-Ukrainian_war_(2022%E2%80%93present)#cite_note-Pulver_Shoard_2024_a031-803]


